00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.580
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
OK, so to begin.

00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:32.549
So in the last class
we talked to them

00:00:32.549 --> 00:00:35.760
about some of the early
documentary films.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.670
The films by Nanook of the
North by Flaherty and Man

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:42.855
With a Movie Camera by Dziga
Vertov, and in today's class

00:00:42.855 --> 00:00:44.730
we're going to be shifting
a bit and thinking

00:00:44.730 --> 00:00:46.772
a bit more about the
question of the relationship

00:00:46.772 --> 00:00:48.990
between documentary
film and science.

00:00:48.990 --> 00:00:54.480
And one of the things that
many commentators argue often

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:56.910
links science and
documentary in terms

00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.500
of people's common
perception of it,

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:00.875
is that there's often a tendency
to think of both of them

00:01:00.875 --> 00:01:06.690
as offering unmediated access
onto the world out there.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:08.190
So there's often
the strong tendency

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:12.030
for people to see documentaries
simply a window onto reality,

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:15.240
rather than being an
interpretation of the reality

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.110
that's out there.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.660
And certainly, that's
the case for science,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:21.030
and an even stronger
sense that for many people

00:01:21.030 --> 00:01:26.700
the idea is that they like to
think of science as allowing

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.120
completely direct access to
the outer physical world.

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:31.590
But many science
studies scholars

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:33.420
would argue that
science doesn't just

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:37.320
simply offer unmediated
access onto the world.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.360
How do scientists
measure or monitor

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.790
or present the natural world?

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:45.390
Often they have to do so
through technical instruments,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.350
for example.

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:50.040
And those instruments serve
as a form of mediation.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:53.310
And when scientists use those
instruments, for example,

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:56.220
or they might use visual
pictures or diagrams based

00:01:56.220 --> 00:01:57.870
on what those
instruments might say,

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:01.950
those visual images also have
to be perceived or interpreted.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.490
And this is
increasingly the case,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.077
given computer simulations.

00:02:05.077 --> 00:02:06.660
So for example, I
have a student who's

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.270
working on the issue
of uranium mining.

00:02:09.270 --> 00:02:11.730
And it used to be the
case that when people--

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.490
when geologists went off to
do work on uranium mining,

00:02:14.490 --> 00:02:21.330
they would go off and do actual
field work at geological sites,

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:26.010
and that would be
the basis of work,

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:27.840
but now increasingly
geologists are

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:30.090
relying on statistical
modeling to find out

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:34.440
where they think that uranium
deposits might be, for example.

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.690
So there's an increasing way
with the use of computers

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:40.110
and visual modeling that
visual images and diagrams

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:42.900
and pictures become
increasingly central to how

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.920
science gets conveyed or talked
about or thought through.

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.670
So if you think about
those visual images,

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:48.613
you always have it.

00:02:48.613 --> 00:02:50.280
How are those visual
images constructed?

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.020
How do people
interpret those images?

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:53.380
How do they think through them?

00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:55.950
So there's all these kinds
of layers of interpretation

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:58.050
and mediation that happened
through those kinds

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.448
of visual images.

00:02:59.448 --> 00:03:01.740
And then for scientists when
they want to talk or write

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.420
about their work, or they
want to convey it to others,

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:06.300
we have to do it
through speaking, right?

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.045
We rely upon the
language that we use.

00:03:09.045 --> 00:03:11.170
And there are certain
conventions then about, well,

00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:14.550
how do you write up the science
that you're engaged in doing?

00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:17.940
How do you persuade
fellow scientists

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:19.210
that this is a good argument?

00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:21.168
What are the conventions
within your discipline

00:03:21.168 --> 00:03:23.940
about what makes a good
or convincing argument?

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.060
So all of these different kinds
of representation and language

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.400
help to mediate how we perceive
and understand the world

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.720
that we're part of.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:35.610
In addition to whatever
our own personal kind

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:37.693
of social positioning or
institutional positioning

00:03:37.693 --> 00:03:38.310
might be.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:40.530
And similarly documentary
film theorists

00:03:40.530 --> 00:03:42.810
would argue that
documentary film also

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.880
doesn't offer just
simply unmediated access

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:45.870
onto the world.

00:03:45.870 --> 00:03:48.330
I mean, documentary films
are about the real world,

00:03:48.330 --> 00:03:51.810
but they're also interpretations
of what that real world is.

00:03:51.810 --> 00:03:54.288
So and again, it's not to
say that some interpretations

00:03:54.288 --> 00:03:56.580
aren't better than others,
because some interpretations

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.380
are better than others.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.750
But filmmakers do-- I
mean obviously filmmakers

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.490
choose their subjects.

00:04:02.490 --> 00:04:04.920
They choose what to
shoot, they choose

00:04:04.920 --> 00:04:06.930
what to exclude
from the frame, they

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.780
choose how to create a
narrative out of those images

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.280
through the process of editing.

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:15.150
And then you also have
the issue of the subjects

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.420
that documentary
filmmakers are filming,

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:21.070
what are those subjects
interpretations of the world?

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:23.363
And then you have the
people who at the end

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:25.530
view the film who have their
interpretations of what

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:26.410
they think is going on.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.380
So you have the subjects
who are being filmed,

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:30.520
their interpretations and
mediations of the world.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.538
You have the filmmaker
creating mediations

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:34.830
of what's going on through
what the filmmaker is doing,

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.538
and then you have the
people who watch it

00:04:36.538 --> 00:04:39.382
at the end who are interpreting
and meeting it at the same end.

00:04:39.382 --> 00:04:41.340
So you have all these
kinds of different layers

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:46.470
of interpretation that all
go on as people make and view

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:47.370
documentary films.

00:04:50.900 --> 00:04:53.480
And so in this class I just
want to briefly, just very

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:56.480
briefly talk about some
social science perspectives

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:58.190
on thinking about science.

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:01.460
And that got referred to in your
reading a bit as STS studies,

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.158
and I know that article--

00:05:03.158 --> 00:05:05.450
there was a kind of an overview
article that I gave you

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:07.992
by Susan [? Silver, ?] who's
the chair of the department here

00:05:07.992 --> 00:05:11.840
actually, and Sophia [? Rus. ?]
I it's a difficult article.

00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:14.330
You're not really expected to--

00:05:14.330 --> 00:05:16.582
most of you are undergraduates,
except Catarina.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:19.993
Catarina has to understand it.

00:05:19.993 --> 00:05:21.410
But for the rest
of you, the point

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:23.420
is just to give you some
sense of the terrain

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.045
of the arguments,
and you're not really

00:05:25.045 --> 00:05:27.920
expected to know all the ins
and outs and details of all

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.600
that literature.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.133
But just suffice to say that
social studies of science,

00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:34.550
it gets called by
different names.

00:05:34.550 --> 00:05:36.350
Science studies, social
studies of science,

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:39.290
STS, lots of other different
names we won't go into,

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:42.290
has been an interdisciplinary
venture by historians,

00:05:42.290 --> 00:05:46.910
philosophers, anthropologists,
sociologists, and others

00:05:46.910 --> 00:05:50.990
that gained considerable
momentum during the 1960s

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:53.180
and '70s, and it's
offered a way to think

00:05:53.180 --> 00:05:55.580
about science in social terms.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:57.800
To stand back a bit
from the science

00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:00.830
and think about what it is in
terms of the ideas at work.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.330
Not only the ideas
at work, but also

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:03.788
in terms of the
people who practice

00:06:03.788 --> 00:06:06.110
that science, the
communities and institutions

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:07.580
that they're part of.

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.893
And these are important
social questions for us

00:06:09.893 --> 00:06:11.060
to be thinking about, right?

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:14.540
Because science and
modern technologies

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.180
are so incredibly crucial
to our contemporary lives.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.980
They shape how we
live, how we think

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:22.650
in the world, the kinds of
policy decisions that get made,

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.650
the health of our bodies,
the kinds of knowledge

00:06:24.650 --> 00:06:25.490
we consider valid.

00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:26.907
I mean so these
are all completely

00:06:26.907 --> 00:06:28.020
central social questions.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.400
So for social scientists
and philosophers,

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:33.200
these kinds of
questions around science

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:35.040
are also extremely important.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:37.340
So it's not only important
for science and engineers,

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.980
but it's also important for
people in other disciplines

00:06:39.980 --> 00:06:42.150
to be thinking about
these kinds of questions.

00:06:42.150 --> 00:06:43.970
And I just want to throw out
a couple of important points

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.290
that science studies
has made that I

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:47.030
think it might be
useful for all of us

00:06:47.030 --> 00:06:48.320
in thinking about this class.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.932
And as you think about
your final projects,

00:06:50.932 --> 00:06:53.390
and you think about what kind
of project do you want to do,

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:57.540
it might help a bit
in thinking about it.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:00.290
So one important insight
from science studies

00:07:00.290 --> 00:07:03.408
is that instead of seeing,
well, here's society over here,

00:07:03.408 --> 00:07:05.450
and here's science over
here, and they're somehow

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.490
separate in opposition
to each other.

00:07:07.490 --> 00:07:09.650
That one of the major
points of science studies

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:12.290
is that you always have
to see science and society

00:07:12.290 --> 00:07:16.440
as being co-produced or in
relationship to each other.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.920
And maybe some of this sounds
a bit obvious, I'm not sure.

00:07:18.920 --> 00:07:23.000
But obviously science comes
out of our social world.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.270
It's part of the social
world in which we exist.

00:07:26.270 --> 00:07:29.150
Science gets produced by people
in particular historical times

00:07:29.150 --> 00:07:30.060
and places.

00:07:30.060 --> 00:07:33.235
And because of the times and
places in which we all exist,

00:07:33.235 --> 00:07:34.610
we ask certain
kinds of questions

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:37.580
and we don't ask other
kinds of questions.

00:07:37.580 --> 00:07:40.250
The time periods in
which we're operating

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.230
shapes what we consider
to be important or not.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:45.990
It shapes what
gets funded or not.

00:07:45.990 --> 00:07:48.230
And science-- as
Susan Sylvia argues,

00:07:48.230 --> 00:07:50.107
science is a communal process.

00:07:50.107 --> 00:07:52.190
For the most part it happens
through institutions.

00:07:52.190 --> 00:07:53.565
It's a collective
enterprise that

00:07:53.565 --> 00:07:57.530
happens at places like MIT,
and in through other kinds

00:07:57.530 --> 00:07:58.730
of institutions.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:02.240
Students get trained in
particular kinds of ways.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:03.950
Again, there's
whole funding bodies

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:06.440
that operate in
certain kinds of ways,

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:08.790
and they fund certain kinds
of issues and not others.

00:08:08.790 --> 00:08:10.670
And so we have to
think of science

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:14.400
as being fundamentally a social
or collective kind of activity.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:14.900
OK?

00:08:14.900 --> 00:08:16.692
And I just want to make
very clear, though,

00:08:16.692 --> 00:08:18.920
that this does not
in any way undermine

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:23.570
the validity or the importance
of the knowledge that comes out

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:24.650
of science.

00:08:24.650 --> 00:08:27.920
And if anything I would argue
that it adds a certain richness

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:29.480
and appreciation to
understanding it,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:32.480
because there's also these whole
layers of social complexity

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:36.150
that are also part
of it as well.

00:08:36.150 --> 00:08:39.929
So that's one crucial point.

00:08:39.929 --> 00:08:44.360
Secondly another crucial
point is that science studies

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:47.420
has shifted even
in its own thinking

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.120
from not only thinking
about the content

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:52.730
of scientific knowledge, but
also to thinking about science

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:54.590
as something that people do.

00:08:54.590 --> 00:08:55.213
Right?

00:08:55.213 --> 00:08:57.380
And so you have a number
of science studies scholars

00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:58.250
that engage--

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:01.867
that have been engaged
in laboratory studies.

00:09:01.867 --> 00:09:03.950
So they go and they spend
time in lab laboratories

00:09:03.950 --> 00:09:05.825
because they want to
pay attention to science

00:09:05.825 --> 00:09:06.560
as a process.

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:08.670
What do scientists actually do?

00:09:08.670 --> 00:09:10.085
How do they know what they know?

00:09:10.085 --> 00:09:11.210
How do they think about it?

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:12.085
How do they learn it?

00:09:12.085 --> 00:09:14.150
How do they train
their students?

00:09:14.150 --> 00:09:18.350
How does this affect the
ways that knowledge ends up

00:09:18.350 --> 00:09:19.650
being produced?

00:09:19.650 --> 00:09:20.150
OK?

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:21.900
And so you have social
scientists scholars

00:09:21.900 --> 00:09:23.630
who do work in laboratories.

00:09:23.630 --> 00:09:25.520
One of the articles
that you read for today

00:09:25.520 --> 00:09:28.250
by Sharon Traweek-- she's
done a very famous ethnography

00:09:28.250 --> 00:09:30.710
called Beamtimes and Lifetimes
where she did research

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:35.150
in laboratories with physicists,
both in the US and in Japan.

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:36.650
Which is quite an
interesting study.

00:09:36.650 --> 00:09:38.750
I sometimes use it in anthro.

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:41.270
It's a good read, if
anybody is interested.

00:09:41.270 --> 00:09:45.320
And then you have other social
science studies scholars

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:49.290
that also look at scientists
beyond the laboratory.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:51.890
So you have Hugh
Gusterson, somebody

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.610
else who used to teach here
at MIT who no longer does.

00:09:55.610 --> 00:09:57.470
He's at George Mason now.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:09:59.690
But he did work with
nuclear weapons designers

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:00.980
at Livermore Lab.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:02.510
And because it
was classified, he

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.450
couldn't get access to
where they were actually

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:06.150
doing the work.

00:10:06.150 --> 00:10:09.710
And so he ended up kind of
lurking around Livermore

00:10:09.710 --> 00:10:12.240
Lab basically on the outside.

00:10:12.240 --> 00:10:14.510
And so he would go to
coffee shops with people,

00:10:14.510 --> 00:10:15.530
go to their houses.

00:10:15.530 --> 00:10:17.420
He would go to church,
he would go shopping.

00:10:17.420 --> 00:10:19.430
And he would do these
really in-depth interviews

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:20.040
with people.

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.730
And so he spent a lot of
time hanging out in the areas

00:10:22.730 --> 00:10:25.520
where nuclear weapons
designers were

00:10:25.520 --> 00:10:27.350
spending time talking to them.

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:31.785
And he became-- it was very
interesting in his work

00:10:31.785 --> 00:10:34.160
from the beginning, it was
kind of like well who are you?

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.345
And then afterwards I think
people came to so enjoy

00:10:38.345 --> 00:10:39.470
telling their story to him.

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.262
Because a lot of people
hadn't had a chance

00:10:41.262 --> 00:10:43.850
to tell their story about what
they did and why they did it

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:46.940
and how they thought about it in
moral in other kinds of terms,

00:10:46.940 --> 00:10:49.705
that it became the thing
to do that everybody

00:10:49.705 --> 00:10:51.080
wanted to be
interviewed by Hugh.

00:10:51.080 --> 00:10:53.790
And so everybody is like, well,
you interviewed everybody else.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:55.457
How come you haven't
interviewed me yet?

00:10:55.457 --> 00:10:57.710
So he kind of became the
resident anthropologist

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:01.262
of all the nuclear weapons
folks at Livermore Lab.

00:11:01.262 --> 00:11:02.720
And then you have
Stefan Helmreich,

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:06.260
who's another science study
scholar here at MIT who

00:11:06.260 --> 00:11:10.340
also, he did work with
marine microbiologists,

00:11:10.340 --> 00:11:13.910
and following them around
not only in laboratories,

00:11:13.910 --> 00:11:17.540
but taking a trip down
in Alvin under the water,

00:11:17.540 --> 00:11:19.130
going to conferences,
other places

00:11:19.130 --> 00:11:20.420
where scientists might go.

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:22.850
So you have a number of science
study scholars doing work

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:25.370
on what is it that
scientists are doing,

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:28.860
and following scientists
around where they go.

00:11:28.860 --> 00:11:30.260
And so the big
question from this

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:31.802
is, how does science
potentially look

00:11:31.802 --> 00:11:35.450
different when we think about
it as something that people do?

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:37.880
Do we think about
science differently when

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:40.520
you think about it as a
process, as an activity,

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:44.600
rather than simply some data
that you get in a textbook?

00:11:44.600 --> 00:11:47.227
If you think about
it as an activity,

00:11:47.227 --> 00:11:49.310
I think a lot of scholars
who do this kind of work

00:11:49.310 --> 00:11:51.320
would argue it actually kind
of opens up and frees up

00:11:51.320 --> 00:11:53.060
a lot of ways of
thinking about science.

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:55.400
And that's particularly
going to be relevant for us,

00:11:55.400 --> 00:11:59.060
since film is a medium
that focuses on activity.

00:12:01.620 --> 00:12:05.870
So the third issue I
wanted to throw out

00:12:05.870 --> 00:12:07.700
is that science study
scholarship has also

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.160
been helpful in thinking
about questions of science

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:17.120
and technology as they travel
throughout our social world,

00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:19.520
even apart from scientists
and other kinds of folks.

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:22.160
So the question of how
do debates about science

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:27.050
spread through other aspects
of not only US society,

00:12:27.050 --> 00:12:28.860
but other regions of the world.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:31.120
So you can think about
this question, say--

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.120
I mean lots of different
ways to think about it.

00:12:33.120 --> 00:12:36.740
But in relation to environmental
pollution is one example.

00:12:36.740 --> 00:12:39.290
So if people live in
a community that they

00:12:39.290 --> 00:12:41.210
have concerns about
environmental pollution,

00:12:41.210 --> 00:12:42.920
science becomes really
crucial in terms

00:12:42.920 --> 00:12:44.598
of trying to figure
out, well, what

00:12:44.598 --> 00:12:45.890
is the nature of the pollution?

00:12:45.890 --> 00:12:46.850
What is the extent of it?

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.017
There might be debates about
what kinds of knowledge

00:12:49.017 --> 00:12:50.657
gets counted or
doesn't get counted.

00:12:50.657 --> 00:12:52.740
But for a lot of people
in their day to day lives,

00:12:52.740 --> 00:12:54.282
I mean science is
obviously something

00:12:54.282 --> 00:12:56.510
that's a crucial part
just for average people

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.592
in terms of living in
the contemporary world.

00:12:58.592 --> 00:13:00.050
And internationally
you think about

00:13:00.050 --> 00:13:02.050
international development,
a lot of the language

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.650
of international
development projects

00:13:03.650 --> 00:13:07.550
uses managerial
language, but also

00:13:07.550 --> 00:13:10.910
language of science, science
and technology as part of it.

00:13:10.910 --> 00:13:12.810
So there's all these
questions about, well,

00:13:12.810 --> 00:13:15.620
what are the discussions
about science and technology

00:13:15.620 --> 00:13:18.380
and all these spaces apart from
laboratories and scientists

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.520
in places like MIT.

00:13:19.520 --> 00:13:22.247
How does it work out
there in the world?

00:13:22.247 --> 00:13:24.830
And part of the reason I think
that's important for this class

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.020
is, again, as you think
about your final projects,

00:13:27.020 --> 00:13:29.870
we're encouraging people
to do final projects

00:13:29.870 --> 00:13:31.460
around science and technology.

00:13:31.460 --> 00:13:33.750
But we also want you to
think broadly about that.

00:13:33.750 --> 00:13:37.250
You don't have to just think
doing science in a lab.

00:13:37.250 --> 00:13:39.390
Although that can be
really fascinating.

00:13:39.390 --> 00:13:41.450
You know, so if you can
think of a good project

00:13:41.450 --> 00:13:44.610
you want to do based on that,
that could be fantastic.

00:13:44.610 --> 00:13:48.020
But if you also want to
follow things outside of MIT,

00:13:48.020 --> 00:13:54.053
or take an unconventional
angle on this, do it.

00:13:54.053 --> 00:13:55.220
You'd be creative with this.

00:13:55.220 --> 00:13:56.810
Think about how
this stuff moves,

00:13:56.810 --> 00:13:58.610
how it goes to different
kinds of places,

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.192
what do people do with it.

00:14:00.192 --> 00:14:02.525
So anyway, so we want you to
be thinking in those terms.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:08.450
OK.

00:14:08.450 --> 00:14:10.580
So and just once again
to emphasize this point

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:12.960
that for us in
thinking of this class,

00:14:12.960 --> 00:14:16.470
we really want to be thinking
about science as a process.

00:14:16.470 --> 00:14:19.610
Not only questions about
knowledge as an end product,

00:14:19.610 --> 00:14:22.557
but a process of what do
scientists do, how do they

00:14:22.557 --> 00:14:24.890
come to know, what are the
relationships that scientists

00:14:24.890 --> 00:14:25.760
are part of.

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.395
How does science and
technology circulate out there?

00:14:28.395 --> 00:14:30.020
What kinds of debates
are they part of?

00:14:30.020 --> 00:14:32.470
What are the implications
of the debates?

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:35.480
Now I lost my next page.

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:36.660
Sorry.

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:37.160
Anyway.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:45.210
Sorry, I had file
conversion problems.

00:14:45.210 --> 00:14:48.320
So I got a bit stuck here.

00:14:48.320 --> 00:14:49.640
Anyway, OK.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:51.200
And I think this
focus on practices,

00:14:51.200 --> 00:14:55.100
again, is useful for
many different reasons.

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:57.680
In addition to thinking
about the film angle.

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:00.990
But also thinking of science
as a kind of practice

00:15:00.990 --> 00:15:04.410
or as a process I think also
helps to make science human.

00:15:04.410 --> 00:15:04.910
Right?

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:08.180
It helps to bring the human
element back in, and make

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:09.680
science more approachable.

00:15:09.680 --> 00:15:12.290
And again, this is also relevant
for thinking about doing

00:15:12.290 --> 00:15:15.950
documentary films about this.

00:15:15.950 --> 00:15:19.082
And it's also focusing on
how people come to have--

00:15:19.082 --> 00:15:21.290
also by focusing on how
people come to have knowledge

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:23.498
that you gain appreciation
for that kind of knowledge

00:15:23.498 --> 00:15:25.710
as well as you see the
limitations of that knowledge.

00:15:25.710 --> 00:15:26.210
Right?

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.890
When you focus on,
well, how are we

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:30.140
going about getting knowledge?

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:32.240
What are we able to get
access to, or what not?

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.680
What's the potential
constraints?

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:35.750
What's the things that
we're really good at?

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:37.220
Isn't it amazing
that people were

00:15:37.220 --> 00:15:39.320
able to figure this
kind of thing out?

00:15:39.320 --> 00:15:40.820
But what's the kind
of stuff that's

00:15:40.820 --> 00:15:41.960
difficult to think about?

00:15:41.960 --> 00:15:44.480
That it's actually by following
these activities of science

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:46.820
that we get a stronger
sense, and again, I

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:49.640
think a richer sense of
what do we know well,

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.980
and what do we know less well.

00:15:54.980 --> 00:15:56.877
And as we've talked
about earlier in class,

00:15:56.877 --> 00:15:58.460
part of the goal of
this class is also

00:15:58.460 --> 00:16:02.190
thinking about documentary
film as a process itself.

00:16:02.190 --> 00:16:04.760
So again, we're not just
looking at documentary films

00:16:04.760 --> 00:16:05.670
as a product.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:06.170
Right?

00:16:06.170 --> 00:16:08.003
We're not just looking
at the final product.

00:16:08.003 --> 00:16:10.490
We're also trying
to think about what

00:16:10.490 --> 00:16:12.290
were the context in
which the films got

00:16:12.290 --> 00:16:15.170
made, what were the social
relationships like that

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:17.690
were part of the
documentary filmmaking, what

00:16:17.690 --> 00:16:19.130
kinds of choices got made.

00:16:19.130 --> 00:16:21.350
Both the aesthetic
choices, but also choices

00:16:21.350 --> 00:16:25.430
between relationships between
subjects and audiences.

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:26.900
What got included in the films?

00:16:26.900 --> 00:16:28.353
What got left out of the films?

00:16:28.353 --> 00:16:30.020
So we're thinking
back to the discussion

00:16:30.020 --> 00:16:32.742
we had about Vertov and
Flaherty so all those questions

00:16:32.742 --> 00:16:33.950
were become important, right?

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:35.450
I mean, what were
the social context

00:16:35.450 --> 00:16:37.340
in which those films were made?

00:16:37.340 --> 00:16:38.330
Who were they made for?

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:40.790
How were choices made, what
were the relationships that

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:43.340
got set up through the
making of documentary film?

00:16:43.340 --> 00:16:45.920
So we're thinking about
both science as a process,

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.420
and we're thinking
about documentary

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:48.548
filmmaking as a process.

00:16:48.548 --> 00:16:50.840
And so the idea of this class
is to put the two of them

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.610
together.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:55.010
So thinking about the process
of documentary filmmaking,

00:16:55.010 --> 00:16:56.510
thinking about
science as a process,

00:16:56.510 --> 00:17:00.120
and hopefully trying to make
videos and films about that.

00:17:00.120 --> 00:17:00.620
OK?

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:03.984
The conjuncture where
they both come together.

00:17:03.984 --> 00:17:05.359
And again, we're
going to go back

00:17:05.359 --> 00:17:07.442
to this at the end of the
class where we talk more

00:17:07.442 --> 00:17:08.660
about your final projects.

00:17:08.660 --> 00:17:13.940
But first we want to show you
some documentary films that

00:17:13.940 --> 00:17:15.780
have been made about science.

00:17:15.780 --> 00:17:17.000
And I just wanted to throw--

00:17:17.000 --> 00:17:19.250
I'm going to be tethered,
so this will be a little bit

00:17:19.250 --> 00:17:23.750
difficult. OK, but as we talked
about in the very first class,

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:25.670
science films have
always been there

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.040
from the very beginning
of filmmaking.

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:32.090
As we saw early on from
those 1905 films by Charles

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:35.570
[? Urban ?] that we discussed.

00:17:35.570 --> 00:17:38.030
So very, very long history.

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:42.200
But we want to list some of
the ways in which filmmaking

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:44.540
and science have come together.

00:17:44.540 --> 00:17:46.220
And I just want to list four.

00:17:46.220 --> 00:17:48.545
And maybe you all can
help me think of others.

00:17:52.010 --> 00:17:55.070
But one major way has been
for some people, anyway,

00:17:55.070 --> 00:17:59.180
to think of a film or video
as a means of collecting data.

00:17:59.180 --> 00:17:59.750
Right?

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:04.040
How can how can we use
film or video as a way

00:18:04.040 --> 00:18:07.140
to collect data, to collect
information about the world?

00:18:07.140 --> 00:18:11.660
So whether it's about using film
or video to slow things down,

00:18:11.660 --> 00:18:13.370
do things in slow
motion that we wouldn't

00:18:13.370 --> 00:18:17.900
be able to see otherwise,
potentially speed things up.

00:18:17.900 --> 00:18:20.270
So a whole range
of what can you do

00:18:20.270 --> 00:18:23.785
with these kinds of technologies
that allows you to see--

00:18:23.785 --> 00:18:25.160
not only allows
you to see things

00:18:25.160 --> 00:18:27.368
in different kinds of ways,
but that you can actually

00:18:27.368 --> 00:18:28.080
use as data.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:30.080
And then there's the whole
range of other things

00:18:30.080 --> 00:18:32.600
that this can lead off
into in the sciences where

00:18:32.600 --> 00:18:34.520
you have all kinds of
imaging techniques,

00:18:34.520 --> 00:18:37.130
where you could be brain
scans, or other kinds of scans.

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:40.550
So that's a whole other
kind of using visual images

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:41.390
as data collection.

00:18:41.390 --> 00:18:43.880
But here we're focusing
on particular kinds

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:48.830
of uses of film and
video as data collecting.

00:18:48.830 --> 00:18:59.220
Another way the two
have been linked

00:18:59.220 --> 00:19:05.130
is this question of how can
we use film or video as a way

00:19:05.130 --> 00:19:07.990
to go out and explore the world?

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:10.360
How can we use
these instruments,

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:14.080
these technologies to
explore these worlds,

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:16.660
these social worlds
around science?

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:20.260
Or questions that science
might raise for us.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:21.160
How do we use them?

00:19:21.160 --> 00:19:23.570
Can we use them as
tools of exploration?

00:19:23.570 --> 00:19:24.070
OK.

00:19:28.180 --> 00:19:30.045
A third way in which
they have been linked

00:19:30.045 --> 00:19:31.420
is an obvious one
that's probably

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.920
familiar to all of
us, is education.

00:19:33.920 --> 00:19:34.420
Right?

00:19:34.420 --> 00:19:35.900
And there's two aspects of this.

00:19:35.900 --> 00:19:41.230
One coming from the
end of the scientists,

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:44.890
and one coming from the
end of the filmmakers.

00:19:44.890 --> 00:19:47.290
OK?

00:19:47.290 --> 00:19:52.180
So a question is then, for
scientists, the question--

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:55.180
one of the main questions about
film and video could be how can

00:19:55.180 --> 00:19:57.130
we use these kinds
of technologies

00:19:57.130 --> 00:20:00.460
in order to educate
people about the sciences.

00:20:00.460 --> 00:20:03.460
And then one of the primary
questions for the scientists

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:06.190
become, how do we take
the work that we've done

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.830
and convey that in visual terms?

00:20:08.830 --> 00:20:11.472
What are the means we
can use to convey this?

00:20:11.472 --> 00:20:13.180
And these are difficult
questions, right?

00:20:13.180 --> 00:20:14.763
And these are also
difficult questions

00:20:14.763 --> 00:20:18.070
for scientists when they write.

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:20.860
Because how do you explain
to other people what

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:23.200
it is that you're working
on, and often scientists

00:20:23.200 --> 00:20:26.920
have to rely upon certain
kinds of written metaphors

00:20:26.920 --> 00:20:29.530
to try to explain what
they're getting at.

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:32.590
Are those metaphors-- I
mean and do the metaphors--

00:20:32.590 --> 00:20:34.797
I mean they never quite
exactly work, right?

00:20:34.797 --> 00:20:36.880
And so one of the questions
that scientists always

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:40.810
to have to think about then is,
is the metaphor that I'm using,

00:20:40.810 --> 00:20:43.300
is it helpful or is it
potentially leading things

00:20:43.300 --> 00:20:44.240
astray?

00:20:44.240 --> 00:20:44.740
OK?

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:46.240
And the same kinds
of questions then

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:48.700
when scientists want to
use visual technologies.

00:20:48.700 --> 00:20:52.510
When you're using film and
video to try to educate people

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:57.100
about the sciences, what kinds
of visual images or narratives

00:20:57.100 --> 00:20:59.820
are you going to use to try
to convey that information.

00:20:59.820 --> 00:21:02.800
And what's the potential
benefits or constraints

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:04.730
about the way that
you choose to do it.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:07.120
So that becomes a major
question for scientists.

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:09.850
For filmmakers one of the
major questions becomes, well,

00:21:09.850 --> 00:21:11.350
what kinds of
narratives can you use

00:21:11.350 --> 00:21:13.017
to talk about the
science that are going

00:21:13.017 --> 00:21:14.660
to keep people interested?

00:21:14.660 --> 00:21:15.160
Right?

00:21:15.160 --> 00:21:17.828
I mean so their
goal also has to be,

00:21:17.828 --> 00:21:19.870
how do you make something
interesting that people

00:21:19.870 --> 00:21:21.430
want to see?

00:21:21.430 --> 00:21:25.390
So it becomes, well, what
interests people about science?

00:21:25.390 --> 00:21:29.290
How do I convey something in a
visually interesting way that

00:21:29.290 --> 00:21:32.590
tells a story in a way
that will bring people

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:35.990
in where they'll want to
learn more about the science?

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:36.490
OK?

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:40.450
So these kinds of questions
about how we use film and video

00:21:40.450 --> 00:21:41.860
as means of education.

00:21:44.120 --> 00:21:44.620
OK.

00:21:51.420 --> 00:21:54.690
And then also certainly
within popular culture,

00:21:54.690 --> 00:21:57.910
there's also just a lot of
fascination with science.

00:21:57.910 --> 00:21:58.410
Right?

00:21:58.410 --> 00:22:01.260
So then there's also using
film and video technologies

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:05.550
as a way to satisfy popular
curiosity about science

00:22:05.550 --> 00:22:08.280
in a way that might not quite
be strictly educational.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.710
So again, we talk to them
about the IMAX films,

00:22:10.710 --> 00:22:12.710
and they might be somewhat
educational, somewhat

00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:13.530
entertainment.

00:22:13.530 --> 00:22:16.230
But in a way that
satisfies public curiosity

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:19.060
about the sciences.

00:22:19.060 --> 00:22:19.740
OK?

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:23.490
And so first we're going to
think about all of these a bit

00:22:23.490 --> 00:22:25.767
as we watch various
clips tonight.

00:22:25.767 --> 00:22:27.600
And the first one we're
going to think about

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:31.950
is using film and video as a
technique for generating data.

00:22:31.950 --> 00:22:34.770
And we're going to be doing
this through the figure of Doc

00:22:34.770 --> 00:22:35.820
Edgerton.

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:40.610
Any of you know who
Doc Edgerton was?

00:22:40.610 --> 00:22:42.760
[? Anita? ?]

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:46.570
AUDIENCE: He has a
[? sense ?] [INAUDIBLE]..

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:49.990
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, so Doc
Edgerton was-- he trained here.

00:22:49.990 --> 00:22:54.160
He was born in 1903,
he died in 1990.

00:22:54.160 --> 00:22:55.060
He was a legend.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:56.260
CHRIS BOEBEL: He died
in the MIT faculty club.

00:22:56.260 --> 00:22:56.650
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Did he really?

00:22:56.650 --> 00:22:57.880
CHRIS BOEBEL: Collapsed
and died, yes.

00:22:57.880 --> 00:22:58.330
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Really?

00:22:58.330 --> 00:22:58.390
Oh, that's horrible.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:22:59.515
CHRIS BOEBEL: In 1990, yes.

00:22:59.515 --> 00:23:00.070
Anyway.

00:23:00.070 --> 00:23:01.480
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Wow.

00:23:01.480 --> 00:23:04.580
He had been a faculty member
here at MIT for 40 years.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.250
He had been here as
an undergraduate.

00:23:06.250 --> 00:23:09.550
He went off and did research
at GE, I believe it was,

00:23:09.550 --> 00:23:13.810
for a few years and then he came
back to do his PhD here at MIT.

00:23:13.810 --> 00:23:15.910
And he became interested--

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:18.430
he was working with motors.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.080
And one of the things
that he noticed

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:22.090
when he was doing his work
that when there were problems

00:23:22.090 --> 00:23:24.310
with electrical transmission
lines and the lights

00:23:24.310 --> 00:23:27.370
would flicker, that he
could observe changes

00:23:27.370 --> 00:23:30.950
in the motion of the motor
that he couldn't otherwise.

00:23:30.950 --> 00:23:33.670
And so what he ended up
doing during the 1930s

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:35.770
was he developed a
repeatable short duration

00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:39.910
electronic flash, or a
stroboscopic light that could

00:23:39.910 --> 00:23:41.540
reveal motion in segments.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:43.810
So you could capture
fast motion in a way

00:23:43.810 --> 00:23:47.650
that was not accessible
to our normal perception.

00:23:47.650 --> 00:23:50.830
So he did-- I mean, I
didn't write it down,

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:54.380
but I think it's in here,
exactly how it worked.

00:23:54.380 --> 00:23:56.470
So Edgerton called his
unit a stroboscope.

00:23:56.470 --> 00:24:00.550
It could emit 60 10-microsecond
flashes per second,

00:24:00.550 --> 00:24:02.870
a hitherto unimaginable speed.

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:03.760
OK?

00:24:03.760 --> 00:24:07.900
So through his
invention Doc Edgerton

00:24:07.900 --> 00:24:12.340
was able to capture
things using photography,

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:15.100
so it's he did both still
photography, but also

00:24:15.100 --> 00:24:17.380
obviously if you do still
photography that quickly

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:20.770
and you run it together,
you have moving images.

00:24:20.770 --> 00:24:25.360
So this is also the intersection
of photography and moving

00:24:25.360 --> 00:24:27.310
pictures.

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:32.860
So he did this
fascinating work where

00:24:32.860 --> 00:24:35.140
he did extraordinarily
famous work, again,

00:24:35.140 --> 00:24:40.270
both in still photography
and in moving images.

00:24:40.270 --> 00:24:43.180
I'm sure many of
you have seen his,

00:24:43.180 --> 00:24:45.880
even if you're not aware
that they were necessarily

00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:47.530
Doc Edgerton's work.

00:24:47.530 --> 00:24:50.560
His work became
extremely famous.

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:54.453
He-- actually there was a short.

00:24:54.453 --> 00:24:56.620
There was a short film that
was made about his work,

00:24:56.620 --> 00:24:59.890
I think it was in the 1940s
that won an Academy Award.

00:24:59.890 --> 00:25:01.300
His work was shown all over.

00:25:01.300 --> 00:25:04.030
And it also crossed the boundary
between science and art,

00:25:04.030 --> 00:25:07.960
where some of his work was just
seen as so gorgeous that it was

00:25:07.960 --> 00:25:10.322
shown in museums as artwork.

00:25:10.322 --> 00:25:12.280
So he was kind of working
on that boundary then

00:25:12.280 --> 00:25:14.650
between the world of science
and the world of art.

00:25:14.650 --> 00:25:18.730
He also did other things as
well with a couple of students

00:25:18.730 --> 00:25:20.270
from here at MIT.

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:22.990
He became involved during
the Second World War

00:25:22.990 --> 00:25:25.370
in classified military work.

00:25:25.370 --> 00:25:29.920
So they did work on inventions
for aerial reconnaissance,

00:25:29.920 --> 00:25:31.120
aerial photography.

00:25:31.120 --> 00:25:36.400
And then also later they
did photographic images

00:25:36.400 --> 00:25:38.770
of nuclear weapons blasts.

00:25:38.770 --> 00:25:40.430
And there's actually
some online,

00:25:40.430 --> 00:25:43.523
you can actually see them if
you're interested in that.

00:25:43.523 --> 00:25:45.190
He worked with Jacques
Cousteau, helping

00:25:45.190 --> 00:25:48.160
Jacques Cousteau develop
underwater camera equipment.

00:25:48.160 --> 00:25:49.910
So he was an inventor.

00:25:49.910 --> 00:25:51.830
He was a scientist.

00:25:51.830 --> 00:25:53.890
He was-- some people
would call him an artist.

00:25:53.890 --> 00:25:56.110
He said, "Don't make
me out to be an artist.

00:25:56.110 --> 00:25:56.860
I'm an engineer.

00:25:56.860 --> 00:25:59.950
I am after the facts,
only the facts."

00:25:59.950 --> 00:26:02.200
But what his work I think
suggests for a lot of people

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:09.350
is, can work that is science
also be art at the same time?

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:11.020
So anyway so let's watch a few.

00:26:11.020 --> 00:26:11.800
CHRIS BOEBEL: So
yeah, I don't know

00:26:11.800 --> 00:26:13.008
which ones you want to watch.

00:26:13.008 --> 00:26:14.960
You should drive.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:36.360
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

00:29:36.360 --> 00:29:38.400
So that was a bit of
an introductory one.

00:31:14.210 --> 00:31:17.210
So here's another one of
this famous milk drop pieces,

00:31:17.210 --> 00:31:19.460
but this is later in color.

00:34:00.990 --> 00:34:04.020
And I'm sure you've seen
some of his famous stills.

00:34:04.020 --> 00:34:06.810
Have seen the one with the
still photograph with the apple

00:34:06.810 --> 00:34:08.370
with a bullet going through it?

00:34:08.370 --> 00:34:10.409
Have ever seen that image?

00:34:10.409 --> 00:34:12.000
You've seen the
photograph, yeah.

00:34:12.000 --> 00:34:16.096
Which he famously titled,
"Making Applesauce at MIT."

00:34:16.096 --> 00:34:18.179
And you might have seen
this famous one of a card,

00:34:18.179 --> 00:34:20.820
with a bullet going through
a card, like still images.

00:34:20.820 --> 00:34:24.880
They were shown them in
publications like Life

00:34:24.880 --> 00:34:25.630
during their time.

00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:28.350
I mean, they really
circulated widely.

00:34:28.350 --> 00:34:31.218
Let me show you a few more.

00:34:31.218 --> 00:34:32.635
AUDIENCE: I think
a couple of them

00:34:32.635 --> 00:34:35.940
are on display in building 34.

00:34:35.940 --> 00:34:37.940
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
I wouldn't be surprised,

00:34:37.940 --> 00:34:42.020
because the 150th anniversary,
there's a lot of interest in--

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:43.520
and I think the MIT Museum is--

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:44.270
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, the museum.

00:34:44.270 --> 00:34:46.010
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Is doing a
Doc Edgerton display right now

00:34:46.010 --> 00:34:47.330
because the 150th anniversary.

00:34:47.330 --> 00:34:50.630
So there's probably a
fair amount of stuff

00:34:50.630 --> 00:34:53.030
around right now.

00:34:53.030 --> 00:34:54.245
Hummingbird.

00:34:54.245 --> 00:34:55.370
This is really pretty, too.

00:34:57.960 --> 00:35:00.317
AUDIENCE: There's a milk
drop poster in Lobby 7.

00:35:00.317 --> 00:35:02.400
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Milk
drop poster in the Lobby 7.

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:04.830
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah that's
right, that's right, yeah.

00:35:04.830 --> 00:35:07.830
And if anybody has any doubt
