00:38:35.490 --> 00:38:35.990
OK.

00:38:46.125 --> 00:38:47.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: Just
watch a few minutes.

00:38:47.750 --> 00:38:49.190
CHRISTINE WALLEY: This
one-- we'll just watch.

00:38:49.190 --> 00:38:50.818
This is the half hour
episode about him

00:38:50.818 --> 00:38:52.610
that gives a little
context, but we'll just

00:38:52.610 --> 00:38:53.990
watch a few minutes of it.

00:39:01.430 --> 00:39:03.680
JOHN FITCH: You are watching
a swimmer taking pictures

00:39:03.680 --> 00:39:05.690
with an underwater camera.

00:39:05.690 --> 00:39:08.450
But below a few hundred
feet, as the light disappears

00:39:08.450 --> 00:39:11.450
and the pressures become too
great for human endurance,

00:39:11.450 --> 00:39:14.090
other techniques for
photographing the silent world

00:39:14.090 --> 00:39:16.970
in the deep oceans
have to be used.

00:39:16.970 --> 00:39:19.280
These techniques for
deep sea photography

00:39:19.280 --> 00:39:22.531
are our story today
on Science Reporter.

00:39:22.531 --> 00:39:25.477
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:39:47.130 --> 00:39:49.890
Hello, I'm John Fitch,
MIT science reporter.

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:52.110
We're at the MIT
swimming pool today

00:39:52.110 --> 00:39:54.720
to talk to a man who has
made major contributions

00:39:54.720 --> 00:39:56.580
to the science of photography.

00:39:56.580 --> 00:39:58.737
He is Dr. Harold
Edgerton, a professor

00:39:58.737 --> 00:40:00.570
of electrical measurements
in the Department

00:40:00.570 --> 00:40:02.730
of Electrical
Engineering at MIT,

00:40:02.730 --> 00:40:05.190
the man who is both an
inventor and a teacher,

00:40:05.190 --> 00:40:08.850
and internationally famous as
the father of modern high speed

00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:10.080
photography.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:13.980
At the edge of 28, Dr. Edgerton
invented an electronic device

00:40:13.980 --> 00:40:17.430
which allowed invisible motion,
much too fast for the human eye

00:40:17.430 --> 00:40:19.650
to see, such as the
swing of a golf club,

00:40:19.650 --> 00:40:22.080
or the path of a
bullet in flight,

00:40:22.080 --> 00:40:25.680
to be frozen onto a
single photographic plate.

00:40:25.680 --> 00:40:27.240
More recently Dr.
Edgerton has been

00:40:27.240 --> 00:40:29.250
adapting his
electronic flash lamps

00:40:29.250 --> 00:40:30.870
to photograph
another world which

00:40:30.870 --> 00:40:32.670
is invisible to the naked eye.

00:40:32.670 --> 00:40:34.680
The world of the ocean depths.

00:40:34.680 --> 00:40:37.800
Some of the equipment designed
by Dr. Edgerton for deep sea

00:40:37.800 --> 00:40:40.800
photography is set up
here at the swimming pool,

00:40:40.800 --> 00:40:42.460
and we're going to
meet Dr. Edgerton

00:40:42.460 --> 00:40:45.750
and learn more about it.

00:40:45.750 --> 00:40:48.450
HAROLD EDGERTON: Everything
worked all right?

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:51.420
Fine, then we'll have it
covered from several angles.

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:52.635
JOHN FITCH: Dr. Edgerton?

00:40:52.635 --> 00:40:54.510
That looks very much
like an ordinary camera.

00:40:54.510 --> 00:40:56.933
What keeps the water out
from ruining the film?

00:40:56.933 --> 00:40:58.350
HAROLD EDGERTON:
Well, this camera

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:02.190
is like an ordinary
camera, except a case.

00:41:02.190 --> 00:41:04.740
[INAUDIBLE] waterproof case.

00:41:04.740 --> 00:41:08.645
It was developed in France by
the captain Jacques Cousteau

00:41:08.645 --> 00:41:10.800
whom I've met many times.

00:41:10.800 --> 00:41:13.050
It's now made in Japan.

00:41:13.050 --> 00:41:17.040
This lens, for
example, comes out,

00:41:17.040 --> 00:41:21.930
and the lens is protected by
rubber rings called O rings

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:25.375
which seal against a surface.

00:41:25.375 --> 00:41:27.000
JOHN FITCH: So how
do you focus and set

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:29.100
the distance and aperture
and all those things?

00:41:29.100 --> 00:41:30.892
HAROLD EDGERTON: When
you turn those knobs,

00:41:30.892 --> 00:41:33.450
you see one of them changes
focus, the other one changes

00:41:33.450 --> 00:41:36.360
the aperture.

00:41:36.360 --> 00:41:37.560
JOHN FITCH: Very clever.

00:41:37.560 --> 00:41:41.710
HAROLD EDGERTON: And this
fits in through the O rings.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:45.240
And that seals.

00:41:45.240 --> 00:41:47.580
Now I'm not going
to take this apart,

00:41:47.580 --> 00:41:49.410
because it has film in here.

00:41:49.410 --> 00:41:53.370
But if you pull these two
levers, the entire mechanism,

00:41:53.370 --> 00:41:56.213
camera mechanism comes out,
and you can get at the film.

00:41:56.213 --> 00:41:57.630
JOHN FITCH: That's
also sealed in?

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:58.590
HAROLD EDGERTON:
It's also sealed in,

00:41:58.590 --> 00:42:00.180
and you trigger
the thing by means

00:42:00.180 --> 00:42:03.630
of a pusher at this point.

00:42:03.630 --> 00:42:04.440
Release.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:06.232
JOHN FITCH: I notice
you also had-- he took

00:42:06.232 --> 00:42:07.440
his own life along with him.

00:42:07.440 --> 00:42:10.890
HAROLD EDGERTON: Yes, of course
every picture must have light,

00:42:10.890 --> 00:42:13.710
and in underwater it's
particularly important

00:42:13.710 --> 00:42:16.740
because the sunlight does
not penetrate too well,

00:42:16.740 --> 00:42:19.172
and you always want
to film in shadows.

00:42:19.172 --> 00:42:20.630
So of course, in
this day and age--

00:42:20.630 --> 00:42:22.547
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Jacques
Cousteau's equipment

00:42:22.547 --> 00:42:25.260
was developed with Doc Edgerton
in the alumni pool right

00:42:25.260 --> 00:42:28.663
over there, which is
where this is shot.

00:42:28.663 --> 00:42:30.330
So it's of those kinds
of amazing things

00:42:30.330 --> 00:42:31.800
about walking around
MIT campus when

00:42:31.800 --> 00:42:33.508
you start to learn
more about the history

00:42:33.508 --> 00:42:35.400
and everything was
going on in these spaces

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:37.817
that we don't really pay that
much attention to as we just

00:42:37.817 --> 00:42:39.353
walk around.

00:42:39.353 --> 00:42:41.020
But we can-- have you
had enough of Doc?

00:42:41.020 --> 00:42:44.700
Or do you want to see a
nuclear explosion or something?

00:42:44.700 --> 00:42:45.930
Or a plant unfolding?

00:42:45.930 --> 00:42:49.795
Or flowers or nuclear explosion.

00:42:49.795 --> 00:42:51.670
CHRIS BOEBEL: The nuclear
explosions-- there.

00:42:51.670 --> 00:42:53.160
Yeah, let's take a quick look.

00:42:53.160 --> 00:42:55.650
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

00:42:55.650 --> 00:42:58.020
CHRIS BOEBEL: Here we go.

00:42:58.020 --> 00:43:01.737
He actually did some really
extraordinary still photos.

00:43:01.737 --> 00:43:03.570
I mean, just images
you've never seen before

00:43:03.570 --> 00:43:07.200
of the first few microseconds
of a nuclear explosion

00:43:07.200 --> 00:43:10.960
when it's still this tiny,
tightly packed ball of energy,

00:43:10.960 --> 00:43:13.350
and it doesn't look like
a nuclear explosion.

00:43:13.350 --> 00:43:15.225
It's really amazing.

00:45:38.665 --> 00:45:39.540
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

00:45:39.540 --> 00:45:41.300
So reactions,
thoughts, comments?

00:45:46.910 --> 00:45:51.270
AUDIENCE: I think it's
kind of interesting how

00:45:51.270 --> 00:45:53.558
allowing the camera
to run faster

00:45:53.558 --> 00:45:56.100
lets you actually slow it down,
because I was trying to think

00:45:56.100 --> 00:45:57.540
like, why does that work?

00:45:57.540 --> 00:45:59.970
But then it's like the
more frames you have,

00:45:59.970 --> 00:46:01.870
when you play them
back at normal speed,

00:46:01.870 --> 00:46:04.947
it moves at a slower pace.

00:46:04.947 --> 00:46:06.780
I just thought that was
interesting in terms

00:46:06.780 --> 00:46:09.573
of what the technology says.

00:46:09.573 --> 00:46:10.490
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yes.

00:46:10.490 --> 00:46:11.323
I'm sorry, go ahead.

00:46:11.323 --> 00:46:14.654
AUDIENCE: I think it's also
cool how he [INAUDIBLE] the milk

00:46:14.654 --> 00:46:18.580
and how it changes the surface
of [INAUDIBLE],, and then

00:46:18.580 --> 00:46:21.540
also [INAUDIBLE]
nuclear explosion,

00:46:21.540 --> 00:46:25.640
pushing [INAUDIBLE]
completely different scale.

00:46:28.770 --> 00:46:29.910
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

00:46:29.910 --> 00:46:31.740
And to this question
of how it works,

00:46:31.740 --> 00:46:34.830
I mean so the
description given here.

00:46:34.830 --> 00:46:37.020
Synchronized with the camera
so that with each flash

00:46:37.020 --> 00:46:38.400
a frame of film was exposed.

00:46:38.400 --> 00:46:40.927
The flashing light decomposed
the phases of movement.

00:46:40.927 --> 00:46:42.510
This is back to when
he was originally

00:46:42.510 --> 00:46:44.730
doing his original
things with the motors.

00:46:44.730 --> 00:46:47.820
Decomposed the phases of
movement of one of his motors

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.010
into 3,000 images per second.

00:46:51.010 --> 00:46:52.770
So at that point it
was a third faster

00:46:52.770 --> 00:46:54.760
than anybody else had
been able to produce.

00:46:54.760 --> 00:46:56.580
And it was fast enough that
when the film was projected

00:46:56.580 --> 00:46:58.497
at normal speed the swift
turning of the motor

00:46:58.497 --> 00:46:59.980
could be studied in slow motion.

00:46:59.980 --> 00:47:05.810
So again, how
exactly it all works.

00:47:05.810 --> 00:47:07.860
Other reactions, thoughts?

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:11.022
AUDIENCE: How aesthetically
appealing it is.

00:47:11.022 --> 00:47:12.480
I mean it's very
interesting to see

00:47:12.480 --> 00:47:14.820
the movement of the wings
of the bird, but most of all

00:47:14.820 --> 00:47:16.518
it's just beautiful.

00:47:16.518 --> 00:47:18.060
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Can science be art?

00:47:20.632 --> 00:47:22.840
[? CHRIS BOEBEL: ?] Well,
that's a really interesting

00:47:22.840 --> 00:47:24.970
relationship between
data and art.

00:47:24.970 --> 00:47:28.990
And it's something that
you see continuing.

00:47:28.990 --> 00:47:32.470
For those of you who
saw Tom Schillings piece

00:47:32.470 --> 00:47:34.570
that he made for
our last class that

00:47:34.570 --> 00:47:36.730
was shown at last
week, the museum.

00:47:36.730 --> 00:47:39.100
One of the things among
others that strikes me

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:44.380
about some of the AFM nano
images that are in that piece

00:47:44.380 --> 00:47:46.400
is how beautiful they are.

00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:50.310
And so if you watch the
piece, you start to wonder,

00:47:50.310 --> 00:47:52.810
are they beautiful because it
just happened to be beautiful,

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:55.210
or are they beautiful
because the people making

00:47:55.210 --> 00:47:57.400
them and designing the
machines that create them

00:47:57.400 --> 00:48:01.090
that are these analogs
to something that's real

00:48:01.090 --> 00:48:03.970
have a bias towards
making them beautiful?

00:48:03.970 --> 00:48:05.810
AUDIENCE: This reminds
me of-- there's

00:48:05.810 --> 00:48:09.430
a museum in London, the
Wellcome Collection.

00:48:09.430 --> 00:48:11.380
And there was an
exhibition where

00:48:11.380 --> 00:48:15.590
there was a competition
on the kind of imaging

00:48:15.590 --> 00:48:20.320
of bodily tissues, and
kind of medical imaging,

00:48:20.320 --> 00:48:21.460
and the competition.

00:48:21.460 --> 00:48:26.080
And there was an exhibition
of the best 15 works selected,

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:29.110
and they were showing
tissue with cancer

00:48:29.110 --> 00:48:30.760
and all sorts of
very intimate kind

00:48:30.760 --> 00:48:33.760
of horrible details
of disease, but it was

00:48:33.760 --> 00:48:35.677
like an aesthetic competition.

00:48:35.677 --> 00:48:37.760
These were selected for
their aesthetic qualities.

00:48:37.760 --> 00:48:39.460
And it's kind of incredible.

00:48:39.460 --> 00:48:42.250
So you walk into the collection,
and you didn't really

00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:45.888
know what you were
looking at, or how you're

00:48:45.888 --> 00:48:46.930
supposed to relate to it.

00:48:46.930 --> 00:48:48.597
CHRIS BOEBEL: I think
the Koch Institute

00:48:48.597 --> 00:48:52.310
is doing something similar with
their lobby display area there.

00:48:52.310 --> 00:48:55.270
They've had a competition
of bioimaging,

00:48:55.270 --> 00:48:58.240
and it's being judged
on certainly not

00:48:58.240 --> 00:49:02.020
just scientific merit,
but beauty and aesthetics.

00:49:02.020 --> 00:49:03.520
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
And I wonder if it

00:49:03.520 --> 00:49:09.690
is a shift that's happening
more broadly, whereas I think

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:12.520
a few years back as people--
when people were trained

00:49:12.520 --> 00:49:17.050
I think there was a strong
bias that if you had tendencies

00:49:17.050 --> 00:49:18.460
towards those
artistic direction,

00:49:18.460 --> 00:49:20.287
that somehow undermined
your science.

00:49:20.287 --> 00:49:22.120
And you come-- so you
kind of had to choose.

00:49:22.120 --> 00:49:23.170
You couldn't be
one or the other,

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:25.545
or you weren't seen as being
credible in one realm if you

00:49:25.545 --> 00:49:28.900
were seen as being credible
in the other realm.

00:49:28.900 --> 00:49:30.490
But I wonder if that
is breaking down

00:49:30.490 --> 00:49:32.680
a bit with the kinds of
new imaging technologies

00:49:32.680 --> 00:49:35.767
that you have, which are
incredibly aesthetically

00:49:35.767 --> 00:49:36.850
pleasing at the same time.

00:49:36.850 --> 00:49:38.975
And I wonder if it's a
similar kind of moment where

00:49:38.975 --> 00:49:41.530
maybe the art and the science
are kind of coming together

00:49:41.530 --> 00:49:47.000
in a way that maybe hasn't
been true in more recent years.

00:49:47.000 --> 00:49:50.630
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
it also comes up

00:49:50.630 --> 00:49:53.940
[INAUDIBLE] how [? specific ?]
knowledge is used

00:49:53.940 --> 00:49:56.130
in relationship with
the studies of violence

00:49:56.130 --> 00:49:58.890
we're watching weapons
of mass destruction

00:49:58.890 --> 00:50:02.790
and how exactly what is
beautiful, but socially

00:50:02.790 --> 00:50:07.410
historical [INAUDIBLE] to
serve the military purposes

00:50:07.410 --> 00:50:09.100
of the violence.

00:50:09.100 --> 00:50:10.580
So I don't know.

00:50:10.580 --> 00:50:11.900
It was a little disturbing.

00:50:11.900 --> 00:50:13.760
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, well, yeah.

00:50:13.760 --> 00:50:17.630
I mean that's-- I mean, so
there's both flowers unfolding,

00:50:17.630 --> 00:50:20.150
and hummingbirds in flight,
and there's also nuclear

00:50:20.150 --> 00:50:22.290
detonations.

00:50:22.290 --> 00:50:22.790
Right?

00:50:22.790 --> 00:50:25.460
So there are-- so there's
both ends of this.

00:50:25.460 --> 00:50:26.930
And then there's
the questions of,

00:50:26.930 --> 00:50:28.868
as this stuff gets
turned into art,

00:50:28.868 --> 00:50:31.160
are you aestheticizing things
like violence, and that's

00:50:31.160 --> 00:50:33.830
partly the question you're
raising [INAUDIBLE] things

00:50:33.830 --> 00:50:36.890
cancers on people's
bodies, right?

00:50:36.890 --> 00:50:38.090
I mean what's the--

00:50:38.090 --> 00:50:41.540
is that something that's
properly aestheticized or not?

00:50:41.540 --> 00:50:45.230
How are you supposed to relate
to those kinds of images?

00:50:45.230 --> 00:50:47.630
Is it appropriate
to look for beauty

00:50:47.630 --> 00:50:50.120
in certain kinds of images when
they're images of violence,

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:52.225
for example.

00:50:52.225 --> 00:50:54.600
So that raises a whole range
of other kinds of questions.

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:57.930
CHRIS BOEBEL: Which are very
present in film, as well.

00:50:57.930 --> 00:51:00.770
I mean, if you think about
the way violence is handled

00:51:00.770 --> 00:51:05.390
in mainstream films, that's
clearly an issue that you may

00:51:05.390 --> 00:51:06.770
or may not be concerned about.

00:51:06.770 --> 00:51:09.200
The aestheticization
of violence.

00:51:09.200 --> 00:51:13.800
And it's something that comes
up in documentaries as well.

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:18.980
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So, any
further thoughts on all this?

00:51:18.980 --> 00:51:19.970
OK, so let's move on.

00:51:19.970 --> 00:51:23.030
So the second major use of
science and documentary images

00:51:23.030 --> 00:51:24.530
that we're going
to talk about today

00:51:24.530 --> 00:51:27.050
is educational, which
we've already mentioned.

00:51:27.050 --> 00:51:29.420
And there's obviously
long been an interest

00:51:29.420 --> 00:51:34.730
in using film and documentary
as a means to educate

00:51:34.730 --> 00:51:36.110
the public about science.

00:51:36.110 --> 00:51:38.570
And as I mentioned before,
one of the questions

00:51:38.570 --> 00:51:41.510
that immediately comes up
from the end of the scientists

00:51:41.510 --> 00:51:45.530
is how do you visually
convey to audiences what it

00:51:45.530 --> 00:51:47.180
is that you want them to get.

00:51:47.180 --> 00:51:50.670
If you want visual media to
serve as a form of education,

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:52.730
how do you translate
things visually

00:51:52.730 --> 00:51:57.230
in a way that actually
will educate your audience

00:51:57.230 --> 00:51:59.130
and get them engage
with the topic?

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:01.213
And so then the next piece
that we're going to see

00:52:01.213 --> 00:52:05.087
is a film that was made in 1960
called Frames of Reference.

00:52:05.087 --> 00:52:06.920
That tries to do this
in an interesting way.

00:52:06.920 --> 00:52:08.837
I'd be curious to see
what your reactions are.

00:52:08.837 --> 00:52:10.730
It was made by Ricky Leacock.

00:52:10.730 --> 00:52:13.430
Ricky Leacock had studied
physics at Harvard,

00:52:13.430 --> 00:52:15.530
and he later became a
documentary filmmaker,

00:52:15.530 --> 00:52:17.540
an extremely famous
documentary filmmaker

00:52:17.540 --> 00:52:18.890
in the direct cinema tradition.

00:52:18.890 --> 00:52:21.650
Actually if you think back in
the first class, the images

00:52:21.650 --> 00:52:24.770
of a primary with
the JFK election,

00:52:24.770 --> 00:52:28.760
he was a camera person for that.

00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:32.030
Extreme-- did a number of very
famous films some of which

00:52:32.030 --> 00:52:33.620
you might have seen pieces of.

00:52:33.620 --> 00:52:36.590
He taught here at
MIT for many years.

00:52:36.590 --> 00:52:40.770
There was a film program at MIT
that was extremely influential.

00:52:40.770 --> 00:52:43.970
It sort of got phased out
when the Media Lab came in.

00:52:43.970 --> 00:52:48.200
But he had been a very
central figure here at MIT.

00:52:48.200 --> 00:52:50.630
But this was a film
that he made very early

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:52.520
on with two other
physicists, and this

00:52:52.520 --> 00:52:54.070
is before he became a--

00:52:57.470 --> 00:52:59.360
he was obviously making
films at this point.

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:02.660
But it was before he went off
and became a direct cinema

00:53:02.660 --> 00:53:03.990
filmmaker.

00:53:03.990 --> 00:53:05.960
So in the early
years right before.

00:53:05.960 --> 00:53:08.253
CHRIS BOEBEL: I think
he was 22, 23 years old.

00:53:08.253 --> 00:53:10.670
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK, so again,
called Frames of Reference

00:53:10.670 --> 00:53:21.280
from 1960.

00:53:21.280 --> 00:53:24.130
Oh, and he also worked
with Flaherty on something.

00:53:24.130 --> 00:53:27.310
He was a camera
person for Flaherty.

00:53:27.310 --> 00:53:33.275
Flaherty ended up doing a
film about rural Louisiana,

00:53:33.275 --> 00:53:35.650
and he ended up being the
camera person for that as well.

00:53:44.082 --> 00:53:47.554
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:54:17.900 --> 00:54:20.220
- We're used to seeing things
from a particular point

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:21.150
of view.

00:54:21.150 --> 00:54:24.510
That is, from a particular
frame of reference.

00:54:24.510 --> 00:54:28.380
And things look different to us
under different circumstances.

00:54:28.380 --> 00:54:29.970
At the moment things look--

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:40.330
you look peculiar.

00:54:42.085 --> 00:54:43.960
- You're upside down.

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:46.690
- No, you're the one
that's upside down.

00:54:46.690 --> 00:54:48.660
- No, you're upside down.

00:54:48.660 --> 00:54:51.022
- Well, I'm not.

00:54:51.022 --> 00:54:53.490
He's the one that's
upside down, isn't he?

00:54:53.490 --> 00:54:54.680
- Well, let's toss for it.

00:54:54.680 --> 00:54:55.180
- All right.

00:54:58.560 --> 00:54:59.060
OK.

00:55:04.130 --> 00:55:05.400
- You lose.

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:07.600
He's the one who's
really upside down.

00:55:07.600 --> 00:55:09.600
You better come into my
frame of reference, now.

00:55:21.010 --> 00:55:23.950
- My frame of
reference was inverted

00:55:23.950 --> 00:55:26.350
from what it usually is.

00:55:26.350 --> 00:55:28.570
That view of things
would be normal for me

00:55:28.570 --> 00:55:30.460
if I normally
walked on my hands.

00:55:35.620 --> 00:55:38.110
This represents a
frame of reference.

00:55:38.110 --> 00:55:41.800
Just three rods stuck together
so that each is at right angles

00:55:41.800 --> 00:55:43.820
to the other two.

00:55:43.820 --> 00:55:48.110
Now I'm going to move
in this direction.

00:55:48.110 --> 00:55:50.410
You see the frame at the
same spot on your screen,

00:55:50.410 --> 00:55:53.380
but you know I'm moving this way
because we see the wall moving

00:55:53.380 --> 00:55:55.030
this way behind me.

00:55:55.030 --> 00:55:57.470
But how do you know that
I'm not standing still,

00:55:57.470 --> 00:55:58.390
and the wall moving?

00:56:02.510 --> 00:56:04.600
It was the wall that was moving.

00:56:08.920 --> 00:56:10.750
Now the wall has
disappeared and you

00:56:10.750 --> 00:56:14.140
have no way of telling
whether I am moving or not.

00:56:14.140 --> 00:56:16.720
But now you know
that I'm moving.

00:56:16.720 --> 00:56:20.140
The point of this is that
all motion is relative.

00:56:20.140 --> 00:56:23.950
In both cases I was moving
relative to the wall,

00:56:23.950 --> 00:56:27.100
and the wall was
moving relative to me.

00:56:32.370 --> 00:56:34.290
- All motion is
relative, but we tend

00:56:34.290 --> 00:56:36.420
to think of one
thing as being fixed

00:56:36.420 --> 00:56:38.970
and the other thing
as being moving.

00:56:38.970 --> 00:56:42.090
We usually think of the Earth
as fixed and walls are usually

00:56:42.090 --> 00:56:43.680
fixed to the Earth,
so perhaps you

00:56:43.680 --> 00:56:46.260
were surprised the first time
when it was the wall that

00:56:46.260 --> 00:56:49.200
was moving and not Doctor Hume.

00:56:49.200 --> 00:56:51.300
A frame of reference
fixed to the Earth

00:56:51.300 --> 00:56:53.370
is the most common
frame of reference

00:56:53.370 --> 00:56:57.790
in which to observe the
motion of other things.

00:56:57.790 --> 00:57:00.270
This is the frame of
reference that you're used to.

00:57:00.270 --> 00:57:02.890
The frame is fastened
to the table.

00:57:02.890 --> 00:57:05.130
The table is bolted
to the floor.

00:57:05.130 --> 00:57:06.870
The floor is anchored
in the building,

00:57:06.870 --> 00:57:10.530
and the building is firmly
attached to the Earth.

00:57:10.530 --> 00:57:13.200
Of course, the reason
for having three rods

00:57:13.200 --> 00:57:16.770
is the position of any
object such as this ball

00:57:16.770 --> 00:57:21.720
can be specified using
these three reference lines.

00:57:21.720 --> 00:57:25.830
This reference line points in
the direction which we call up,

00:57:25.830 --> 00:57:27.360
which is a different
direction here

00:57:27.360 --> 00:57:29.700
than it is on the other
side of the Earth.

00:57:29.700 --> 00:57:33.690
And these two reference
lines specify a plane, which

00:57:33.690 --> 00:57:37.230
we call horizontal or level.

00:57:37.230 --> 00:57:38.700
In this film,
we're going to look

00:57:38.700 --> 00:57:42.660
at the motion of objects in
this Earth frame of reference,

00:57:42.660 --> 00:57:46.170
and in other frames of reference
moving in different ways

00:57:46.170 --> 00:57:48.750
relative to the Earth frame.

00:57:48.750 --> 00:57:51.900
Well, let's look at a motion.

00:57:51.900 --> 00:57:57.480
This steel ball can be held
up by the electromagnet.

00:57:57.480 --> 00:57:59.520
Now I'm going to open
the switch and you

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:02.580
watch the motion of the ball.

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:05.730
The ball is accelerated
straight down by gravity

00:58:05.730 --> 00:58:09.000
along a line parallel to
this vertical reference line.

00:58:14.680 --> 00:58:16.710
As you can see,
the electromagnet

00:58:16.710 --> 00:58:18.892
is mounted on a
cart that can move,

00:58:18.892 --> 00:58:20.850
and I'm going to do
exactly the same experiment

00:58:20.850 --> 00:58:23.850
that Dr. Hume did, but
this time while the car is

00:58:23.850 --> 00:58:25.980
moving at a constant velocity.

00:58:25.980 --> 00:58:28.660
The cart is pulled
along by a string which

00:58:28.660 --> 00:58:31.080
is wound around this
phonograph turntable,

00:58:31.080 --> 00:58:33.180
and that pulls it with
a constant velocity.

00:58:38.070 --> 00:58:43.770
When the cart passes
this line the ball

00:58:43.770 --> 00:58:47.250
is released, as you can see.

00:58:47.250 --> 00:58:50.160
I'm going to start the cart
down at the end of the table

00:58:50.160 --> 00:58:52.440
so that by the time it
gets to this point I

00:58:52.440 --> 00:58:54.810
can be sure it's moving
with a constant velocity.

00:58:54.810 --> 00:58:57.402
Now I want you to watch
right here so that you

00:58:57.402 --> 00:58:58.485
will see the ball falling.

00:59:12.630 --> 00:59:14.910
I think you can see that
the ball landed in exactly

00:59:14.910 --> 00:59:16.590
the same position
that it did before,

00:59:16.590 --> 00:59:19.560
when Dr. Hume did the
experiment with the cart fixed.

00:59:19.560 --> 00:59:22.597
But this time the ball could
not have fallen straight down.

00:59:22.597 --> 00:59:23.430
Let me show you why.

00:59:27.060 --> 00:59:30.960
The ball was released
at that point.

00:59:30.960 --> 00:59:34.530
If it had fallen straight
down, because the cart moves on

00:59:34.530 --> 00:59:36.120
in the time that
it takes the ball

00:59:36.120 --> 00:59:37.830
would have landed
back here somewhere.

00:59:37.830 --> 00:59:40.350
But it didn't.

00:59:40.350 --> 00:59:43.160
I'm going to do the
experiment again,

00:59:43.160 --> 00:59:44.970
and this time I'm
going to let you

00:59:44.970 --> 00:59:47.490
watch the motion
through a slow motion

00:59:47.490 --> 00:59:52.680
camera which is fixed here.

00:59:52.680 --> 00:59:56.070
As the cart moves by,
the ball will fall,

00:59:56.070 --> 00:59:57.960
and you can watch in
the slow motion camera.

01:00:07.730 --> 01:00:08.980
I'll show you this again.

01:00:08.980 --> 01:00:10.730
This time there will
be a line on the film

01:00:10.730 --> 01:00:11.938
so that you can see the path.

01:00:14.760 --> 01:00:17.400
I think that you can see
that the path of the ball

01:00:17.400 --> 01:00:20.800
is a parabola.

01:00:20.800 --> 01:00:23.470
But all of this has been
in a frame of reference

01:00:23.470 --> 01:00:24.760
fixed to the Earth.

01:00:24.760 --> 01:00:27.670
How would this motion look
in a frame of reference which

01:00:27.670 --> 01:00:29.290
was moving along with the cart?

01:00:32.230 --> 01:00:34.325
Frame of reference like that.

01:00:34.325 --> 01:00:36.200
Well, so that you can
see what it looks like,

01:00:36.200 --> 01:00:41.080
I'm going to fix this
slow motion camera

01:00:41.080 --> 01:00:42.400
so that it moves with the cart.

01:00:47.140 --> 01:00:48.550
Like this.

01:00:48.550 --> 01:00:50.350
I'm going to do the
experiment again.

01:00:50.350 --> 01:00:52.183
And incidentally I'll
start it, and then I'm

01:00:52.183 --> 01:00:54.820
going to stand here so
that when the ball falls,

01:00:54.820 --> 01:00:58.450
you will have something which
is fixed as a reference point.

01:01:19.490 --> 01:01:21.410
In a moving frame
of reference I think

01:01:21.410 --> 01:01:23.420
you could see that
the path of the ball

01:01:23.420 --> 01:01:25.130
is a vertical straight line.

01:01:25.130 --> 01:01:26.600
It looks exactly
the same as it did

01:01:26.600 --> 01:01:31.010
before when Doctor Hume did the
experiment with the cart fixed.

01:01:31.010 --> 01:01:34.070
If we were moving along
in this frame of reference

01:01:34.070 --> 01:01:36.090
and we couldn't see
the surroundings,

01:01:36.090 --> 01:01:38.510
then we wouldn't be able
to tell by this experiment

01:01:38.510 --> 01:01:40.370
that we were moving at
a constant velocity.

01:01:40.370 --> 01:01:41.720
As a matter of
fact, we wouldn't be

01:01:41.720 --> 01:01:43.512
able to tell by any
experiment that we were

01:01:43.512 --> 01:01:46.130
moving at a constant velocity.

01:01:46.130 --> 01:01:48.090
I'm going to do the
experiment once more.

01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:51.110
And this time I'm not going
to stand here behind the ball

01:01:51.110 --> 01:01:54.440
as it falls so that you won't
have any fixed reference frame.

01:02:11.250 --> 01:02:13.260
As far as you're
concerned that time,

01:02:13.260 --> 01:02:16.380
the cart wasn't
necessarily moving at all.

01:02:16.380 --> 01:02:18.893
That time when you couldn't
see the background,

01:02:18.893 --> 01:02:20.310
then I think perhaps
it was harder

01:02:20.310 --> 01:02:24.548
for you to realize that you were
in a moving frame of reference.

01:02:24.548 --> 01:02:25.802
[END PLAYBACK]

01:02:25.802 --> 01:02:27.510
CHRIS BOEBEL: So if
anybody's interested,

01:02:27.510 --> 01:02:30.030
this is available online.

01:02:30.030 --> 01:02:33.960
And this goes on
quite interesting,

01:02:33.960 --> 01:02:38.160
lots of great little
toys and experience.

01:02:38.160 --> 01:02:40.827
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
reactions, comments?

01:02:40.827 --> 01:02:42.660
AUDIENCE: This is a
slightly random comment,

01:02:42.660 --> 01:02:44.580
but there was a speck
of paint on the wall.

01:02:44.580 --> 01:02:46.350
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:02:46.350 --> 01:02:48.960
AUDIENCE: So you could
see that, yeah, moving.

01:02:48.960 --> 01:02:50.580
Yeah.

01:02:50.580 --> 01:02:52.050
I just thought I'd throw it out.

01:02:52.050 --> 01:02:53.790
CHRIS BOEBEL: Now,
I noticed that too.

01:02:53.790 --> 01:02:57.390
It's funny because one of the
things I love about this film

01:02:57.390 --> 01:02:59.515
is that they have such
great rigs and toys

01:02:59.515 --> 01:03:01.140
and they've set it
all up and they make

01:03:01.140 --> 01:03:02.880
it really, really easy seeming.

01:03:02.880 --> 01:03:04.922
So well, put the
little screw in here,

01:03:04.922 --> 01:03:06.630
and now the cameras
attached to the cart.

01:03:06.630 --> 01:03:08.700
And the phonograph
turntable will turn it

01:03:08.700 --> 01:03:09.610
at a constant speed.

01:03:09.610 --> 01:03:11.640
So it all seems
really effortless,

01:03:11.640 --> 01:03:13.890
and the whole rig of
the guy upside down

01:03:13.890 --> 01:03:15.450
and the camera spinning around.

01:03:15.450 --> 01:03:17.520
But yeah, the speck
of paint is just

01:03:17.520 --> 01:03:18.960
a little detail they missed.

01:03:23.500 --> 01:03:29.680
AUDIENCE: Kind of looks like
these kind of TV chefs program

01:03:29.680 --> 01:03:30.790
there.

01:03:30.790 --> 01:03:32.170
They have all their money.

01:03:32.170 --> 01:03:36.010
Looks really-- so who
was this made for?

01:03:36.010 --> 01:03:38.320
CHRIS BOEBEL: It was--
it's an educational film.

01:03:38.320 --> 01:03:41.800
It was made with grants.

01:03:41.800 --> 01:03:44.022
Grant funding and,
do you know the year?

01:03:44.022 --> 01:03:44.980
CHRISTINE WALLEY: 1960.

01:03:44.980 --> 01:03:46.060
CHRIS BOEBEL: 1960, yeah.

01:03:46.060 --> 01:03:47.247
AUDIENCE: And for schools?

01:03:47.247 --> 01:03:48.580
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, for schools.

01:03:50.877 --> 01:03:52.960
CHRISTINE WALLEY: To educate
people about physics.

01:03:52.960 --> 01:03:53.800
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

01:03:53.800 --> 01:03:58.330
I think it's the
whole idea of laying

01:03:58.330 --> 01:04:01.315
the foundation for relativity
and just understanding

01:04:01.315 --> 01:04:04.930
how speeds are--

01:04:04.930 --> 01:04:10.750
objects are either fixed
or not fixed depending

01:04:10.750 --> 01:04:12.032
on where the observer is.

01:04:12.032 --> 01:04:13.990
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
what it's very famous for

01:04:13.990 --> 01:04:15.760
is that at the beginning.

01:04:15.760 --> 01:04:20.632
The opening with-- as you can
imagine, which is quite a--

01:04:20.632 --> 01:04:22.840
I mean so that's something
to think about, too, then,

01:04:22.840 --> 01:04:25.000
as you go to think
about making films,

01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:29.050
not that you're expected
to put that much effort.

01:04:29.050 --> 01:04:31.190
But they were trying
to think about, well,

01:04:31.190 --> 01:04:35.590
how would you convey something
in a visually arresting way

01:04:35.590 --> 01:04:38.457
to a general audience
that would really

01:04:38.457 --> 01:04:40.540
make the point that they're
talking about in terms

01:04:40.540 --> 01:04:41.710
of frames of reference.

01:04:41.710 --> 01:04:43.780
And I mean it is kind of a
brilliant way to get into it,

01:04:43.780 --> 01:04:45.310
and it takes an
incredible amount of work.

01:04:45.310 --> 01:04:47.935
I mean you think about how much
work they put into it, as Chris

01:04:47.935 --> 01:04:51.310
said, setting up all of these
rigs to actually convey that.

01:04:51.310 --> 01:04:53.750
But the kind of forethought
that goes into, well,

01:04:53.750 --> 01:04:56.800
how do you convey what
you want to convey,

01:04:56.800 --> 01:04:59.500
if you want to educate someone
about these kinds of concepts?

01:04:59.500 --> 01:05:02.770
How would you translate
that individual terms?

01:05:02.770 --> 01:05:06.550
CHRIS BOEBEL: And the way
certain visual images persist.

01:05:06.550 --> 01:05:09.350
Everybody who sees this film
remembers that beginning.

01:05:09.350 --> 01:05:11.050
You may not remember
anything else,

01:05:11.050 --> 01:05:13.390
but everybody
remembers that opening.

01:05:15.960 --> 01:05:19.257
AUDIENCE: I think very
effective because--

01:05:19.257 --> 01:05:20.590
CHRISTINE WALLEY: It is perfect.

01:05:20.590 --> 01:05:23.350
AUDIENCE: It's effective the
first thing, because you--

01:05:23.350 --> 01:05:26.090
I mean if we see
something like that.

01:05:26.090 --> 01:05:28.970
You wouldn't think that that's
actually, that that's real.

01:05:28.970 --> 01:05:30.440
[INAUDIBLE] that's just a trick.

01:05:30.440 --> 01:05:33.650
But I was confused, like um.

01:05:33.650 --> 01:05:38.042
But it doesn't-- it's very
old, it can't be a trick?

01:05:38.042 --> 01:05:39.342
How did they do it?

01:05:39.342 --> 01:05:40.550
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right, exactly.

01:05:40.550 --> 01:05:41.150
Yeah.

01:05:41.150 --> 01:05:42.692
AUDIENCE: That's
because this is old?

01:05:42.692 --> 01:05:44.330
Do we really expect it?

01:05:44.330 --> 01:05:47.648
But right now
[INAUDIBLE] whatever.

01:05:47.648 --> 01:05:49.190
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Right, true enough.

01:05:49.190 --> 01:05:52.040
It wouldn't carry quite
the same cachet today.

01:05:52.040 --> 01:05:52.700
It's true.

01:05:52.700 --> 01:05:54.492
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
that's why, even then I

01:05:54.492 --> 01:05:59.870
mean they had some ability
to manipulate space and do

01:05:59.870 --> 01:06:01.440
composites and so on.

01:06:01.440 --> 01:06:04.400
But that's why you have to pull
out and see the quarter fall

01:06:04.400 --> 01:06:06.230
and see him flip around.

01:06:06.230 --> 01:06:10.820
It's very much a
magician showing you

01:06:10.820 --> 01:06:13.760
how it's done, rather than
just having it stay magic.

01:06:17.195 --> 01:06:18.820
AUDIENCE: Very simple
comment, the fact

01:06:18.820 --> 01:06:21.970
that they're talking
to you, and that you

01:06:21.970 --> 01:06:24.160
are at the same time,
of course, to watch

01:06:24.160 --> 01:06:27.618
the film, but also watching
what's happening in the film,

01:06:27.618 --> 01:06:29.410
and I think these are
two different things.

01:06:32.550 --> 01:06:39.060
And it's definitely an
effective educational strategy.

01:06:39.060 --> 01:06:40.820
CHRIS BOEBEL: It's
also kind of--

01:06:40.820 --> 01:06:42.440
it's obvious but
it's worth saying

01:06:42.440 --> 01:06:46.980
that it's an interesting
example of the camera is you.

01:06:46.980 --> 01:06:48.980
I mean of course, they're
talking to the camera,

01:06:48.980 --> 01:06:51.770
but it's also ride
along on the cart.

01:06:51.770 --> 01:06:54.470
And the camera goes on the cart,
and then you're on the cart.

01:06:54.470 --> 01:06:55.460
You are the camera.

01:06:55.460 --> 01:06:56.420
You, the audience.

01:06:56.420 --> 01:06:59.090
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] because
you are a different camera.

01:06:59.090 --> 01:07:01.253
Sometimes so you see the
camera and then you enter.

01:07:01.253 --> 01:07:03.170
CHRIS BOEBEL: And then
you become that camera.

01:07:03.170 --> 01:07:04.010
Yes, you do switch.

01:07:04.010 --> 01:07:05.510
Sometimes you're a
different camera.

01:07:08.420 --> 01:07:11.110
AUDIENCE: It also on that
point, it's also interesting

01:07:11.110 --> 01:07:15.100
that throughout the
whole piece you--

01:07:15.100 --> 01:07:17.627
like from the very-- after
the first scene then you start

01:07:17.627 --> 01:07:19.210
thinking, oh, I'm
going to be tricked.

01:07:19.210 --> 01:07:21.340
I need to pay attention.

01:07:21.340 --> 01:07:23.270
Think about what's happening.

01:07:23.270 --> 01:07:26.590
And I think that's also
really effective in engaging

01:07:26.590 --> 01:07:28.280
the audience.

01:07:28.280 --> 01:07:31.020
It also just makes
it really interesting

01:07:31.020 --> 01:07:33.160
because you're like, it's
kind of like a puzzle.

01:07:33.160 --> 01:07:34.118
Trying to figure out.

01:07:34.118 --> 01:07:35.410
What are they going to do next.

01:07:35.410 --> 01:07:37.480
Like I can't be tricked.

01:07:37.480 --> 01:07:39.850
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
well, and you think back

01:07:39.850 --> 01:07:43.240
to Flaherty, again, when
he was doing the igloo,

01:07:43.240 --> 01:07:46.792
and when Nanook does the
window, and he doesn't tell you

01:07:46.792 --> 01:07:48.250
immediately what's
going on, you're

01:07:48.250 --> 01:07:50.658
supposed to watch
and figure it out.

01:07:50.658 --> 01:07:52.450
So that's one of the
things that filmmakers

01:07:52.450 --> 01:07:55.150
do use very effectively
to pull audiences in,

01:07:55.150 --> 01:07:57.910
is to allow the audience to
be able to discover things

01:07:57.910 --> 01:08:00.250
through the process of
filmmaking without just telling

01:08:00.250 --> 01:08:01.090
them everything.

01:08:01.090 --> 01:08:03.940
Or the idea here that you better
pay attention because there's

01:08:03.940 --> 01:08:05.390
going to be something going on.

01:08:05.390 --> 01:08:07.390
There's going to be some
kind of trick involved.

01:08:07.390 --> 01:08:11.620
Again, how do you
engage your audience

01:08:11.620 --> 01:08:15.200
and get them and emotionally
involved in what's going on.

01:08:15.200 --> 01:08:18.319
And sometimes revealing
is the way to do it.

01:08:18.319 --> 01:08:20.850
And sometimes not
revealing certain things

01:08:20.850 --> 01:08:21.850
can be the way to do it.

01:08:21.850 --> 01:08:23.975
CHRIS BOEBEL: Making it
like a puzzle, as you said.

01:08:23.975 --> 01:08:26.620
CHRISTINE WALLEY: As you said,
exactly, making like a puzzle.

01:08:26.620 --> 01:08:29.229
CHRIS BOEBEL: And then
just one last thing.

01:08:29.229 --> 01:08:32.397
Another very small thing
that I think is cool.

01:08:32.397 --> 01:08:34.689
We talked in the first class
about the plastic elements

01:08:34.689 --> 01:08:36.399
of film, and all of
the different things

01:08:36.399 --> 01:08:38.149
you can change and
techniques you can use,

01:08:38.149 --> 01:08:40.930
and obviously there's a lot
of interesting rigging going

01:08:40.930 --> 01:08:44.149
on here and camera techniques.

01:08:44.149 --> 01:08:45.939
But one very simple
thing that I think

01:08:45.939 --> 01:08:48.819
is kind of effective
in a good way

01:08:48.819 --> 01:08:51.680
is this prop that they have
of the x, y, and z-axis.

01:08:51.680 --> 01:08:54.130
And so to show you
what frame of reference

01:08:54.130 --> 01:08:56.319
you're going to be in next,
they put it on the cart.

01:08:56.319 --> 01:08:58.010
They put it on the table.

01:08:58.010 --> 01:09:00.640
And it's just a very,
very simple prop.

01:09:00.640 --> 01:09:03.760
It's a very simple
idea, but it really

01:09:03.760 --> 01:09:05.410
works because it
kind of prepares you

01:09:05.410 --> 01:09:10.939
for the next world you're
going to enter as the camera.

01:09:10.939 --> 01:09:14.170
And it's just a nice use of
a prop as a plastic element.

01:09:14.170 --> 01:09:15.910
You could have made
the film without it,

01:09:15.910 --> 01:09:20.830
but it would have just been that
much more slightly confusing

01:09:20.830 --> 01:09:24.040
as to what frame of reference
you were actually in.

01:09:24.040 --> 01:09:26.740
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And so if you
end up for your final projects,

01:09:26.740 --> 01:09:29.229
end up doing a film
on a project where

01:09:29.229 --> 01:09:38.770
you want to convey some of
the ideas behind the science,

01:09:38.770 --> 01:09:42.130
or whatever it is that you're
going to be doing your work on,

01:09:42.130 --> 01:09:43.537
if it ends up being a topic--

01:09:43.537 --> 01:09:46.120
some kind of topic like this,
it becomes one of the questions.

01:09:46.120 --> 01:09:47.529
Becomes, how do
you convey things

01:09:47.529 --> 01:09:50.920
in a way that's both
accurate and insightful

01:09:50.920 --> 01:09:52.370
and compelling to the viewer.

01:09:52.370 --> 01:09:52.870
Right?

01:09:52.870 --> 01:09:55.090
So how do you be accurate
to the science of what

01:09:55.090 --> 01:09:56.440
you're trying to convey?

01:09:56.440 --> 01:09:58.630
How do you do it in a
way that's insightful,

01:09:58.630 --> 01:10:02.050
that allows the
audience to go a-ha,

01:10:02.050 --> 01:10:04.660
I got that, and that
and that pulls them in.

01:10:04.660 --> 01:10:05.740
And that's tricky.

01:10:05.740 --> 01:10:07.520
That's not an easy thing to do.

01:10:07.520 --> 01:10:12.340
And it requires a fair
amount of thinking about how

01:10:12.340 --> 01:10:14.830
you want to go about doing it.

01:10:14.830 --> 01:10:17.538
And so, OK, so this kind
of educational video

01:10:17.538 --> 01:10:20.080
obviously is from the point of
view of the scientists wanting

01:10:20.080 --> 01:10:23.075
to convey and to educate,
and I mentioned also

01:10:23.075 --> 01:10:24.700
you have to think
about these questions

01:10:24.700 --> 01:10:26.575
also from the point of
view of the filmmaker.

01:10:26.575 --> 01:10:28.825
So again, from the filmmaker,
how do you convey things

01:10:28.825 --> 01:10:31.283
in a way-- and obviously what
we're talking about that here

01:10:31.283 --> 01:10:32.970
too with Ricky
Leacock, how did he--

01:10:32.970 --> 01:10:36.400
presumably he was discussing
with the scientists

01:10:36.400 --> 01:10:38.650
about-- the physicists
about how do exactly

01:10:38.650 --> 01:10:40.858
do we want to convey this
in a way that's compelling.

01:10:40.858 --> 01:10:42.317
But we're going to
be talking later

01:10:42.317 --> 01:10:44.350
in class when we get to
Errol Morris and some

01:10:44.350 --> 01:10:48.160
of the other things about
four filmmakers making films

01:10:48.160 --> 01:10:49.660
about science and scientists.

01:10:49.660 --> 01:10:53.140
What kinds of narratives do
they want to draw on, again,

01:10:53.140 --> 01:10:54.380
as a way to pull people in.

01:10:54.380 --> 01:10:56.672
So we're going to come back
to those kinds of questions

01:10:56.672 --> 01:10:59.360
a little bit later in the class.

01:10:59.360 --> 01:11:03.680
But the third thing that we want
to talk about in today's class,

01:11:03.680 --> 01:11:06.040
a third major use of
science and documentary,

01:11:06.040 --> 01:11:09.640
is to satisfy partly popular
fascination with science.

01:11:09.640 --> 01:11:12.640
And again, all these
things can overlap too,

01:11:12.640 --> 01:11:14.650
so the educational
aspect can overlap

01:11:14.650 --> 01:11:18.655
with the popular
interest in science.

01:11:21.160 --> 01:11:24.820
And again, around this question
of what are some of the stories

01:11:24.820 --> 01:11:28.500
that audiences find fascinating
and want to hear about science.

01:11:28.500 --> 01:11:32.290
And Chris and I
had spent some time

01:11:32.290 --> 01:11:35.170
trying to think through what
are some of the various ways.

01:11:40.550 --> 01:11:42.145
Can I have the chalk?

01:11:52.900 --> 01:11:55.000
I think it's supposed
to go that way.

01:11:55.000 --> 01:12:00.612
CHRIS BOEBEL: [INAUDIBLE]

01:12:00.612 --> 01:12:02.320
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
again, this question

01:12:02.320 --> 01:12:05.260
of what are some of the
stories that audiences

01:12:05.260 --> 01:12:07.510
find fascinating, and want
to hear about science.

01:12:07.510 --> 01:12:09.220
We've talked about
some of these before.

01:12:09.220 --> 01:12:11.620
There's the element
of spectacle.

01:12:11.620 --> 01:12:13.480
And again, think
about IMAX movies

01:12:13.480 --> 01:12:16.060
where, again, the
science is a way

01:12:16.060 --> 01:12:18.820
to see things about the world
in the way we wouldn't do it

01:12:18.820 --> 01:12:22.480
before, and particularly in
ways that are very much visually

01:12:22.480 --> 01:12:27.620
arresting and influencing people
on a kind of physical level.

01:12:27.620 --> 01:12:30.255
So spectacle like IMAX movies.

01:12:30.255 --> 01:12:32.380
CHRIS BOEBEL: Or flies
juggling balls [INAUDIBLE]..

01:12:32.380 --> 01:12:35.065
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Flies juggling
balls, as we saw earlier.

01:12:38.890 --> 01:12:40.810
Another narrative that
audiences are often

01:12:40.810 --> 01:12:44.110
interested in as science
as hero, scientist as hero.

01:12:44.110 --> 01:12:50.170
So there's often a fascination
with scientists and thinking

01:12:50.170 --> 01:12:55.450
about, well, how did a scientist
heroically go off and discover

01:12:55.450 --> 01:12:57.190
something, for
example, that had had

01:12:57.190 --> 01:12:59.600
a strong impact upon the world.

01:12:59.600 --> 01:13:01.660
So that's a common
kind of narrative

01:13:01.660 --> 01:13:03.430
that audiences
are interested in.

01:13:03.430 --> 01:13:11.760
Another one is science
as detective story

01:13:11.760 --> 01:13:13.710
where audiences are
often fascinated

01:13:13.710 --> 01:13:16.530
with how do things get
figured out, thinking

01:13:16.530 --> 01:13:18.510
of it like a detective story.

01:13:18.510 --> 01:13:21.120
Some people are interested
in thinking about science

01:13:21.120 --> 01:13:27.030
in terms of what's the
relationship between reason

01:13:27.030 --> 01:13:28.800
and emotion.

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:32.100
With science often
stereotyped as this

01:13:32.100 --> 01:13:33.558
being solely about
reason, and what

01:13:33.558 --> 01:13:36.058
are the kind of quandaries when
it comes up against emotion.

01:13:36.058 --> 01:13:38.220
And obviously the
quintessential example of this

01:13:38.220 --> 01:13:41.010
is the early Star Trek movies,
where you have Spock, right?

01:13:41.010 --> 01:13:44.430
I mean so Spock is all about
the tension between trying

01:13:44.430 --> 01:13:46.920
to be solely about
reason, but having

01:13:46.920 --> 01:13:49.373
emotional feelings
at the same time,

01:13:49.373 --> 01:13:51.540
and how does he deal with
that kind of relationship.

01:13:51.540 --> 01:13:55.800
And often those
kinds of quandaries

01:13:55.800 --> 01:13:57.420
get talked about
in popular culture

01:13:57.420 --> 01:14:02.270
through a science figure
in there somewhere.

01:14:02.270 --> 01:14:03.810
Man, there's too many cords.

01:14:06.860 --> 01:14:07.360
OK.

01:14:18.550 --> 01:14:22.300
And then there's also
an interest in science

01:14:22.300 --> 01:14:24.070
almost for the
bizarre, where science

01:14:24.070 --> 01:14:26.260
itself becomes kind of a
science fiction about, wow,

01:14:26.260 --> 01:14:28.390
look at this crazy stuff
that science is up to.

01:14:28.390 --> 01:14:31.690
It's even crazier than the
actual science fiction.

01:14:31.690 --> 01:14:33.620
It's so crazy you
couldn't even make it up.

01:14:33.620 --> 01:14:34.630
I mean you've heard
that kind of line

01:14:34.630 --> 01:14:36.790
often I think in popular
discussions about science.

01:14:36.790 --> 01:14:38.530
So a kind of
fascination with science

01:14:38.530 --> 01:14:42.100
about how strange
and odd it can be,

01:14:42.100 --> 01:14:44.100
even stranger than things
that could be made up.

01:14:44.100 --> 01:14:46.308
So this is kind of the list
that we had come up with.

01:14:46.308 --> 01:14:47.560
Can you guys think of others?

01:14:47.560 --> 01:14:56.673
Sort of popular ways that people
relate to sides or stories

01:14:56.673 --> 01:14:58.090
that they're
interested in hearing

01:14:58.090 --> 01:14:59.500
about science or scientists?

01:15:02.020 --> 01:15:06.400
AUDIENCE: I guess maybe stories
that have a moral value,

01:15:06.400 --> 01:15:09.760
that maybe science
discovering something

01:15:09.760 --> 01:15:12.670
causes you to question
the morality of it,

01:15:12.670 --> 01:15:13.960
or whether it's [INAUDIBLE].

01:15:13.960 --> 01:15:15.370
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:15:15.370 --> 01:15:17.820
Do you have a specific
example you're thinking of?

01:15:17.820 --> 01:15:20.950
AUDIENCE: I was
thinking Jurassic Park.

01:15:20.950 --> 01:15:26.020
They learn to bring back
to this ancient animal,

01:15:26.020 --> 01:15:26.905
but is that right?

01:15:28.840 --> 01:15:31.090
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right so
the kind of ethical issues,

01:15:31.090 --> 01:15:32.590
and the kind of
ethical issues then.

01:15:32.590 --> 01:15:36.260
That issue, issues around
cloning, about, oh,

01:15:36.260 --> 01:15:38.260
and actually I think we
had had it on this list.

01:15:38.260 --> 01:15:41.320
And I forgot to put it up, but
the kind of moral quandaries

01:15:41.320 --> 01:15:43.420
around scientists playing God.

01:15:43.420 --> 01:15:46.780
Like when is it OK?

01:15:46.780 --> 01:15:50.260
To push when is it OK
to push human knowledge

01:15:50.260 --> 01:15:51.760
and what people can
do and when does

01:15:51.760 --> 01:15:53.860
it cross a line
into something that

01:15:53.860 --> 01:15:58.060
is potentially morally suspect.

01:15:58.060 --> 01:16:00.130
Yeah, exactly where
is the line that we

01:16:00.130 --> 01:16:01.270
want to draw here or not.

01:16:01.270 --> 01:16:04.000
So that kind of fascination
with those kinds of stories.

01:16:04.000 --> 01:16:05.830
How do we think about
that relationship?

01:16:05.830 --> 01:16:06.582
Sabrina?

01:16:06.582 --> 01:16:08.290
AUDIENCE: Coolness
factor of personality?

01:16:08.290 --> 01:16:10.390
Like sort of like Brian
Greene in the [INAUDIBLE]

01:16:10.390 --> 01:16:11.890
[? universe ?] is
very entertaining.

01:16:11.890 --> 01:16:15.435
The person presenting it is
trying to turn [INAUDIBLE]..

01:16:15.435 --> 01:16:17.810
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah, that's
kind of a subset of scientists

01:16:17.810 --> 01:16:18.520
as hero.

01:16:18.520 --> 01:16:21.190
Scientists as cool.

01:16:21.190 --> 01:16:22.240
The science is cool.

01:16:22.240 --> 01:16:24.018
Yeah.

01:16:24.018 --> 01:16:25.435
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Science as cool.

01:16:28.130 --> 01:16:30.450
And a focus on-- that's
supposed to be as cool.

01:16:33.430 --> 01:16:36.230
And then also the focus
on particular kind

01:16:36.230 --> 01:16:38.030
of charismatic
individuals who are

01:16:38.030 --> 01:16:40.670
seen as conveying a certain
kind of image of science

01:16:40.670 --> 01:16:43.030
that people want to respond to.

01:16:43.030 --> 01:16:44.342
[? Anahita. ?]

01:16:44.342 --> 01:16:46.550
AUDIENCE: Do you think about
some examples of science

01:16:46.550 --> 01:16:51.090
as the superior to human
being the [INAUDIBLE]

01:16:51.090 --> 01:16:57.480
like in the movie
[INAUDIBLE] 2001

01:16:57.480 --> 01:17:01.920
when the robots are the
science [INAUDIBLE]..

01:17:01.920 --> 01:17:04.655
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So when does
science become the villain?

01:17:04.655 --> 01:17:07.030
AUDIENCE: Right, when science
become smarter than humans,

01:17:07.030 --> 01:17:10.062
or overcomes the
human [? subjects. ?]

01:17:10.062 --> 01:17:11.770
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So
and again, that kind

01:17:11.770 --> 01:17:15.460
of question of when
does science become

01:17:15.460 --> 01:17:18.040
about human hubris, where
it surpasses itself,

01:17:18.040 --> 01:17:20.650
and then the technology
or the science

01:17:20.650 --> 01:17:23.250
becomes a danger back
to humans themselves.

01:17:23.250 --> 01:17:28.960
And so you would see that around
in the 1950s when you think

01:17:28.960 --> 01:17:32.440
about all the pop
culture movies that

01:17:32.440 --> 01:17:35.020
were kind of veiled
or overt references

01:17:35.020 --> 01:17:37.420
to nuclear technologies,
or other kinds of things.

01:17:37.420 --> 01:17:40.300
That with people struggling with
the question of when do things

01:17:40.300 --> 01:17:43.987
go beyond a certain
point, where the worry

01:17:43.987 --> 01:17:45.820
becomes about the science
itself coming back

01:17:45.820 --> 01:17:46.990
and its impact on people.

01:17:46.990 --> 01:17:48.490
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
more recently, you

01:17:48.490 --> 01:17:51.400
have a whole the
Matrix franchise,

01:17:51.400 --> 01:17:54.430
the Terminator franchise,
which are about AI

01:17:54.430 --> 01:17:58.150
and technologies that
become their own creators

01:17:58.150 --> 01:18:01.660
and then reject the human
beings that created them.

01:18:01.660 --> 01:18:03.657
And attempt to destroy them.

01:18:03.657 --> 01:18:04.740
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Sabrina?

01:18:04.740 --> 01:18:06.615
AUDIENCE: Science as
like a competition, when

01:18:06.615 --> 01:18:09.860
you have different people trying
to do the same thing and making

01:18:09.860 --> 01:18:11.422
it seem like [INAUDIBLE].

01:18:15.890 --> 01:18:18.590
CHRISTINE WALLEY: A fascination
with science as competition,

01:18:18.590 --> 01:18:21.920
about which becomes part
of the discovery process,

01:18:21.920 --> 01:18:24.170
in a way, except you've got
the added element of drama

01:18:24.170 --> 01:18:28.520
with competition, about who's
going to crack the DNA code,

01:18:28.520 --> 01:18:32.480
or what were these kinds of
scientific competitions about.

01:18:32.480 --> 01:18:35.510
How do people discover
things, and that added element

01:18:35.510 --> 01:18:36.750
of drama.

01:18:36.750 --> 01:18:40.340
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
kind of science

01:18:40.340 --> 01:18:42.695
as creating history,
and creating the future.

01:18:45.740 --> 01:18:48.860
So a lot of representations
of new things there,

01:18:48.860 --> 01:18:52.145
as if the future has been
brought to the present.

01:18:54.305 --> 01:18:55.430
CHRISTINE WALLEY: I think--

01:18:58.580 --> 01:19:06.100
so science almost as a way
for people to think about

01:19:06.100 --> 01:19:08.650
the future and the possibilities
that the future might bring,

01:19:08.650 --> 01:19:14.860
or to [INAUDIBLE] Yeah, I
think that happens a lot,

01:19:14.860 --> 01:19:18.040
when in popular cultural
representations when people

01:19:18.040 --> 01:19:21.880
think about their nervousness or
concerns or worries about what

01:19:21.880 --> 01:19:22.540
the future--

01:19:22.540 --> 01:19:24.040
or their hopes for
that matter, too,

01:19:24.040 --> 01:19:25.457
about what the
future might bring.

01:19:25.457 --> 01:19:27.640
Often that future is
expressed through a reference

01:19:27.640 --> 01:19:29.440
to science and technology
and what it might

01:19:29.440 --> 01:19:33.065
do, rather than other kinds
of social issues per se,

01:19:33.065 --> 01:19:35.440
because, I mean, again, I mean
how do we know necessarily

01:19:35.440 --> 01:19:36.490
what the future is
going to be about?

01:19:36.490 --> 01:19:38.243
But science and
technology is often

01:19:38.243 --> 01:19:40.660
used to represent what we think
the future is going to be.

01:19:43.990 --> 01:19:46.270
So yeah, so the way--

01:19:46.270 --> 01:19:49.120
it's the symbolic connotations,
the symbolic relationship

01:19:49.120 --> 01:19:51.640
that it has to certain
kinds of ideas about time.

01:19:51.640 --> 01:19:54.923
And seen as being representative
of the future. [? Anahita? ?]

01:19:54.923 --> 01:19:56.590
AUDIENCE: I mean,
[INAUDIBLE] little bit

01:19:56.590 --> 01:20:00.040
with the idea of
[INAUDIBLE],, but also creates

01:20:00.040 --> 01:20:02.590
that kind of hierarchy of
power [INAUDIBLE] when you

01:20:02.590 --> 01:20:03.940
see the science fiction movie.

01:20:03.940 --> 01:20:08.500
And especially I think it's more
relevant to the younger people,

01:20:08.500 --> 01:20:12.153
like feeling that all
this is the scientist

01:20:12.153 --> 01:20:13.570
[INAUDIBLE] hierarchy
at all, they

01:20:13.570 --> 01:20:17.530
have all these resources, and
this position to [INAUDIBLE]

01:20:17.530 --> 01:20:21.370
be heroes for the future
or do all this cool things.

01:20:21.370 --> 01:20:23.802
But here I am looking at it.

01:20:23.802 --> 01:20:25.510
So it's like it's a
hierarchy between you

01:20:25.510 --> 01:20:28.810
and whatever is going on.

01:20:28.810 --> 01:20:31.720
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So a
kind of concern with, OK,

01:20:31.720 --> 01:20:34.900
who are these people who have
this authority, who are making

01:20:34.900 --> 01:20:37.540
these kinds of decisions, that
I don't necessarily have input

01:20:37.540 --> 01:20:40.847
into, and are they people
doing this for the greater

01:20:40.847 --> 01:20:42.430
good in a way that's
going to help me,

01:20:42.430 --> 01:20:45.370
or is this potentially
very-- the decisions

01:20:45.370 --> 01:20:46.870
that they're going
to make are going

01:20:46.870 --> 01:20:49.120
to be extremely problematic.

01:20:49.120 --> 01:20:50.360
AUDIENCE: Science is power.

01:20:50.360 --> 01:20:52.990
So people who have the science
are the powerful people,

01:20:52.990 --> 01:20:56.140
and if I want to be powerful,
I have to have the science.

01:20:56.140 --> 01:20:57.765
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
kind of the narrative

01:20:57.765 --> 01:20:59.020
of the disconnected scientist.

01:20:59.020 --> 01:21:01.690
The scientist who-- which is,
again, a little like science

01:21:01.690 --> 01:21:02.800
versus emotion.

01:21:02.800 --> 01:21:05.410
The scientist who stands
apart, who has this power,

01:21:05.410 --> 01:21:08.350
but who doesn't understand
what the power can do,

01:21:08.350 --> 01:21:10.060
or it doesn't have
a relationship

01:21:10.060 --> 01:21:12.970
to people, average
people who are going

01:21:12.970 --> 01:21:15.857
to be affected by something.

01:21:15.857 --> 01:21:17.440
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, I mean I think

01:21:17.440 --> 01:21:20.023
all this is really important to
get out, because there's both,

01:21:20.023 --> 01:21:22.060
again, what people
on the science end

01:21:22.060 --> 01:21:23.980
might want to convey
to the public,

01:21:23.980 --> 01:21:26.770
and then there's also
on the public end

01:21:26.770 --> 01:21:30.550
what is the fascination
with the scientists,

01:21:30.550 --> 01:21:32.710
and what are the concerns
and quandaries and things

01:21:32.710 --> 01:21:35.560
that people are interested
in thinking about

01:21:35.560 --> 01:21:36.880
in relation to science.

01:21:36.880 --> 01:21:39.070
And obviously, it's not
just the separate thing.

01:21:39.070 --> 01:21:40.900
We have the scientists over
here, pop culture over here.

01:21:40.900 --> 01:21:42.400
I mean one of the things
that's really fascinating when

01:21:42.400 --> 01:21:44.020
we talk about this
later in class

01:21:44.020 --> 01:21:48.118
is if you talk to
many people at MIT,

01:21:48.118 --> 01:21:49.660
and actually this
is going to come up

01:21:49.660 --> 01:21:51.770
in relation to the next
film we're going to see.

01:21:51.770 --> 01:21:53.830
But if you talk to
many people around MIT

01:21:53.830 --> 01:21:56.410
of a certain
generation, they might

01:21:56.410 --> 01:22:01.403
have gotten interested in doing
what they do because maybe they

01:22:01.403 --> 01:22:03.820
were really fascinated with
Star Trek when they were kids.

01:22:03.820 --> 01:22:07.780
And they decided, air astro, I
mean that's where I want to go.

01:22:07.780 --> 01:22:09.280
And so a lot of
times if you go back

01:22:09.280 --> 01:22:10.905
and you talk to
people, what originally

01:22:10.905 --> 01:22:13.000
got interested in
thinking about this,

01:22:13.000 --> 01:22:16.060
often people will refer back to
pop cultural images from when

01:22:16.060 --> 01:22:18.340
they were a kid about
whether it's, again,

01:22:18.340 --> 01:22:20.650
Star Trek or actually this--

01:22:20.650 --> 01:22:23.500
this is actually a good
segue into this next video

01:22:23.500 --> 01:22:25.658
that we're going to see,
because according to Chris

01:22:25.658 --> 01:22:27.700
a number of people that
he's talked to around MIT

01:22:27.700 --> 01:22:28.930
mentioned this.

01:22:28.930 --> 01:22:33.700
This series of Connections
had been on the BBC

01:22:33.700 --> 01:22:36.310
as something that very
much influenced them

01:22:36.310 --> 01:22:39.670
when they were younger, when
they decided that they wanted

01:22:39.670 --> 01:22:41.950
to become scientists.

01:22:41.950 --> 01:22:47.360
So OK, so I'll use that
as a segue into this.

01:22:47.360 --> 01:22:50.470
And so this series,
Connections that I just

01:22:50.470 --> 01:22:53.560
mentioned, it's mostly
in that kind of genre

01:22:53.560 --> 01:22:55.090
of science as detective story.

01:22:55.090 --> 01:22:57.548
I mean obviously, it's also
meant to be partly educational,

01:22:57.548 --> 01:22:59.290
but also science
as detective story.

01:22:59.290 --> 01:23:02.530
It's a series that came out in
the late 1970s and the narrator

01:23:02.530 --> 01:23:05.290
is this guy James Burke.

01:23:05.290 --> 01:23:07.210
He was an Irishman who
was trained at Oxford,

01:23:07.210 --> 01:23:09.760
and he became a science
journalist at the BBC.

01:23:09.760 --> 01:23:13.180
And so this ended up
becoming a very famous series

01:23:13.180 --> 01:23:15.160
on science and technology.

01:23:15.160 --> 01:23:19.360
And so we're going to watch part
of the first episode of this.

01:23:19.360 --> 01:23:21.940
He was often described as
being kind of the Sherlock

01:23:21.940 --> 01:23:23.470
Holmes of science.

01:23:26.567 --> 01:23:28.400
OK, I'm not going to
say any more than that.

01:23:28.400 --> 01:23:31.270
I'll just let you respond
and see what you all.

01:23:31.270 --> 01:23:35.017
CHRIS BOEBEL: And I'll just say,
try to think about this film,

01:23:35.017 --> 01:23:37.600
and I mean all the other films
that we've been seeing as well,

01:23:37.600 --> 01:23:40.270
but this one in particular from
the point of view of narrative

01:23:40.270 --> 01:23:41.080
strategies.

01:23:41.080 --> 01:23:44.680
Keeping the audience
engaged and how

01:23:44.680 --> 01:23:50.760
that relates to the information
that's being imparted.

01:23:50.760 --> 01:23:53.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
again, this series

01:23:53.130 --> 01:23:55.770
was an extremely
popular series, and they

01:23:55.770 --> 01:23:57.630
had a lot of funding to do it.

01:23:57.630 --> 01:24:04.860
But extremely popular series
that was quite influential.

01:24:04.860 --> 01:24:06.480
CHRIS BOEBEL: Yeah.

01:24:06.480 --> 01:24:09.390
As Chris mentioned, if you
talk to a lot of professors

01:24:09.390 --> 01:24:16.760
in their 40s who were
teenagers when this came out,

01:24:16.760 --> 01:24:20.630
a lot of them will say, oh I
saw, Connections on PBS when

01:24:20.630 --> 01:24:21.920
I was a kid, and that was it.

01:24:21.920 --> 01:24:24.200
I wanted to be an
engineer or a scientist.

01:24:24.200 --> 01:24:25.160
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
again, this tendency.

01:24:25.160 --> 01:24:26.210
We often have a
tendency to think

01:24:26.210 --> 01:24:28.220
of like, well science is
the serious stuff over here,

01:24:28.220 --> 01:24:29.928
and like the pop
culture stuff is the not

01:24:29.928 --> 01:24:31.110
serious stuff over here.

01:24:31.110 --> 01:24:32.990
But again, as human
beings we get influenced

01:24:32.990 --> 01:24:34.190
by all of this stuff, right?

01:24:34.190 --> 01:24:36.290
And so the pop culture
representations

01:24:36.290 --> 01:24:37.717
become part of
who we are and why

01:24:37.717 --> 01:24:39.800
we're interested in these
questions to begin with,

01:24:39.800 --> 01:24:42.380
and they might be what pushes
people into a certain direction

01:24:42.380 --> 01:24:44.040
to ask certain
kinds of questions.

01:24:44.040 --> 01:24:47.840
So in a way, it's
kind of what we

01:24:47.840 --> 01:24:49.447
want to be thinking
about both ends

01:24:49.447 --> 01:24:51.780
and the potential relationship
that's out there as well.

01:24:51.780 --> 01:24:54.890
And we actually have a class
later on in the semester

01:24:54.890 --> 01:24:57.800
that specifically on science
fiction that's partly

01:24:57.800 --> 01:25:00.770
around these kind of very kinds
of questions about how science

01:25:00.770 --> 01:25:03.590
moves over in to the pop
culture realm, and the influence

01:25:03.590 --> 01:25:07.412
back and forth between science
and the world of fiction.

01:25:07.412 --> 01:25:08.870
CHRIS BOEBEL: But
here you're going

01:25:08.870 --> 01:25:11.310
to see a science documentary
that, among other things,

01:25:11.310 --> 01:25:15.200
is very heavily influenced
by the world of fiction

01:25:15.200 --> 01:25:16.740
filmmaking.

01:25:16.740 --> 01:25:17.240
OK?

01:25:21.208 --> 01:25:23.688
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:25:53.944 --> 01:25:56.920
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:26:10.435 --> 01:26:12.280
- Would you do me a favor?

01:26:12.280 --> 01:26:14.420
I'd like to stop
talking for a minute,

01:26:14.420 --> 01:26:17.260
and when I do, take a
look at the room you're

01:26:17.260 --> 01:26:19.750
in, and above all at
the man made objects

01:26:19.750 --> 01:26:21.760
in that room that surround you.

01:26:21.760 --> 01:26:24.490
The television set, the
lights, the phone, and so on.

01:26:24.490 --> 01:26:28.060
And ask yourself what those
objects do to your life

01:26:28.060 --> 01:26:30.628
just because they're there.

01:26:30.628 --> 01:26:31.606
Go ahead.

01:26:41.880 --> 01:26:46.140
Well, that is what this series
is going to be all about.

01:26:46.140 --> 01:26:48.020
It's about the things
that surround you

01:26:48.020 --> 01:26:50.780
in the modern world, and just
because they're there shape

01:26:50.780 --> 01:26:53.270
the way you think and behave.

01:26:53.270 --> 01:26:56.030
And why they exist
in the form they do.

01:26:56.030 --> 01:27:00.760
And who or what was responsible
for them existing at all.

01:27:00.760 --> 01:27:04.300
The search for those clues will
take us all over the world,

01:27:04.300 --> 01:27:06.850
and 12,000 years into the past.

01:27:06.850 --> 01:27:10.420
Because it's in those strange
places and in those long gone

01:27:10.420 --> 01:27:14.410
centuries that the secret
of the modern world lies.

01:27:14.410 --> 01:27:16.510
And you'll never believe
the extraordinary things

01:27:16.510 --> 01:27:20.070
that led to us being
the way we are today.

01:27:20.070 --> 01:27:24.060
Things like, for instance,
why a 16th century

01:27:24.060 --> 01:27:25.800
doctor at the Court
of Queen Elizabeth

01:27:25.800 --> 01:27:27.810
did something that made
it possible for you

01:27:27.810 --> 01:27:31.250
to watch this screen now.

01:27:31.250 --> 01:27:33.530
Or the fact that because
18th century merchants were

01:27:33.530 --> 01:27:38.440
worried about ship's bottoms,
you have nylon to wear.

01:27:38.440 --> 01:27:40.630
Or why a group of French
monks and their involvement

01:27:40.630 --> 01:27:42.938
with sheep rearing helped
to give the modern world

01:27:42.938 --> 01:27:43.480
the computer.

01:27:46.240 --> 01:27:48.610
Or what medieval Europeans
did with their fire

01:27:48.610 --> 01:27:53.110
in winter that led to
motorcar manufacture.

01:27:53.110 --> 01:27:55.180
[CHANTING]

01:27:59.740 --> 01:28:03.250
The story of the events and the
people who over centuries came

01:28:03.250 --> 01:28:05.230
together to bring
us in from the cold

01:28:05.230 --> 01:28:07.690
and to wrap us in a warm
blanket of technology

01:28:07.690 --> 01:28:10.510
is a matter of vital
importance, since more and more

01:28:10.510 --> 01:28:13.150
of that technology infiltrates
every aspect of our lives.

01:28:13.150 --> 01:28:14.950
It's become a life
support system

01:28:14.950 --> 01:28:17.060
without which we can't survive.

01:28:17.060 --> 01:28:21.430
And yet how much of
it do we understand?

01:28:21.430 --> 01:28:23.050
Do I bother myself
with the reality

01:28:23.050 --> 01:28:25.360
of what happens when I
get into a big steel box,

01:28:25.360 --> 01:28:28.330
press the button, and
rise into the sky?

01:28:28.330 --> 01:28:31.196
Of course, I don't.

01:28:31.196 --> 01:28:34.010
[END PLAYBACK]

01:28:36.095 --> 01:28:37.845
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK,
reactions, comments?

01:28:41.020 --> 01:28:42.880
AUDIENCE: So it's
like a reenactment,

01:28:42.880 --> 01:28:46.996
but it's like so theatrical
that it's really interesting.

01:28:52.700 --> 01:28:54.450
AUDIENCE: The pace at
which it's narrated,

01:28:54.450 --> 01:28:57.630
it's very fast and
anxious, and like when he

01:28:57.630 --> 01:28:59.730
was talking about the count--

01:28:59.730 --> 01:29:01.360
it wasn't necessarily
a countdown,

01:29:01.360 --> 01:29:03.710
but as the minutes were
passing until the blackout was

01:29:03.710 --> 01:29:04.640
happening.

01:29:04.640 --> 01:29:06.470
It just made-- it
just emphasized

01:29:06.470 --> 01:29:10.700
like how fast paced the city
was, and then everything just

01:29:10.700 --> 01:29:13.130
stopped and it
slowed down and it

01:29:13.130 --> 01:29:17.870
was as if time froze because
technology wasn't [INAUDIBLE]

01:29:17.870 --> 01:29:19.620
so I kind of like that.

01:29:19.620 --> 01:29:20.930
The way they said that.

01:29:27.577 --> 01:29:29.410
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Other
reactions, comments?

01:29:34.730 --> 01:29:39.770
AUDIENCE: It is really
powerful how it constantly

01:29:39.770 --> 01:29:45.020
takes things that the
audience takes for granted

01:29:45.020 --> 01:29:52.220
and reveals them to be
extraordinary and ephemeral

01:29:52.220 --> 01:30:02.690
and not, yeah, kind of
exoticised them and show them

01:30:02.690 --> 01:30:05.930
in their capacity
of breaking down.

01:30:05.930 --> 01:30:11.120
So it was just kind of
romantic notion of in the end,

01:30:11.120 --> 01:30:14.720
everything is one tiny little
bit can make everything

01:30:14.720 --> 01:30:20.660
else fall apart, and it's
really just looks like a fiction

01:30:20.660 --> 01:30:22.192
movie.

01:30:22.192 --> 01:30:23.150
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:30:23.150 --> 01:30:24.680
So stylistically, right?

01:30:24.680 --> 01:30:26.570
So it's filmed as a--

01:30:29.350 --> 01:30:31.850
well, you also have to see the
relationship between this one

01:30:31.850 --> 01:30:33.980
this, this is the first episode,
and some of the other episodes

01:30:33.980 --> 01:30:34.770
in the series.

01:30:34.770 --> 01:30:37.610
So this one focuses
on New York City

01:30:37.610 --> 01:30:42.320
as the modern
technological metropolis.

01:30:42.320 --> 01:30:46.580
A lot of the other
episodes in the series

01:30:46.580 --> 01:30:49.802
travel to different
parts of the world,

01:30:49.802 --> 01:30:51.510
and in different
historical time periods.

01:30:51.510 --> 01:30:54.340
So it might start off in--

01:30:54.340 --> 01:30:57.410
it might be about the
development of the printing

01:30:57.410 --> 01:30:59.510
press, and it might go
back to the Middle Ages.

01:30:59.510 --> 01:31:02.180
But it's following some
technology historically

01:31:02.180 --> 01:31:04.640
through time, and how
this technology triggered

01:31:04.640 --> 01:31:05.820
all these kinds of impacts.

01:31:05.820 --> 01:31:08.070
And so the idea is to take
things that you would never

01:31:08.070 --> 01:31:11.018
expect and trace
back its genealogy

01:31:11.018 --> 01:31:12.560
and to argue that
while it was linked

01:31:12.560 --> 01:31:14.840
to this kind of
technological transformation

01:31:14.840 --> 01:31:17.060
that happened in this part
of the world and this time

01:31:17.060 --> 01:31:18.920
that you never would
have suspected.

01:31:18.920 --> 01:31:22.250
CHRIS BOEBEL: Basically,
I mean the thesis is just

01:31:22.250 --> 01:31:24.290
as an example,
the second episode

01:31:24.290 --> 01:31:27.230
begins with the
coining of money.

01:31:27.230 --> 01:31:30.770
The first money
several thousand--

01:31:30.770 --> 01:31:34.190
over 2,000 years ago,
and traces this argument

01:31:34.190 --> 01:31:36.980
that the direct result of
that was the development

01:31:36.980 --> 01:31:39.290
of the atomic bomb.

01:31:39.290 --> 01:31:45.710
And traces a technological
evolutionary connection

01:31:45.710 --> 01:31:50.200
argument from one to the other,
with multiple steps in between.

01:31:50.200 --> 01:31:52.730
So the connection
in that instance

01:31:52.730 --> 01:31:55.220
is the connection between
this one tiny accident

01:31:55.220 --> 01:31:56.967
of technology in one
part of the world

01:31:56.967 --> 01:31:59.300
with the next one, with the
next one, with the next one.

01:31:59.300 --> 01:32:01.940
So this is kind an
interesting thematic way

01:32:01.940 --> 01:32:05.870
of getting into it, because
he's looking at a network

01:32:05.870 --> 01:32:08.600
and how one tiny
piece of a network

01:32:08.600 --> 01:32:10.760
can have these
massive repercussions

01:32:10.760 --> 01:32:11.695
across the network.

01:32:11.695 --> 01:32:13.820
Which is exactly what he
then says about technology

01:32:13.820 --> 01:32:15.410
throughout the
rest of the series.

01:32:15.410 --> 01:32:16.880
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So there's
two questions to be asked then.

01:32:16.880 --> 01:32:19.220
I mean, so once you know
where the rest of the series

01:32:19.220 --> 01:32:24.140
is going, and then it set
up as this detective series

01:32:24.140 --> 01:32:27.230
through time and across space.

01:32:27.230 --> 01:32:29.780
How do they convey
aesthetically the sense

01:32:29.780 --> 01:32:31.313
that this is a detective story?

01:32:31.313 --> 01:32:33.230
And if you think back,
I mean, how many of you

01:32:33.230 --> 01:32:36.590
have watched old
film noir movies?

01:32:36.590 --> 01:32:40.880
The film noir genre, the
old black and white where--

01:32:40.880 --> 01:32:44.000
so the shots of him with
half his face in shadow,

01:32:44.000 --> 01:32:45.650
wearing the overcoat.

01:32:45.650 --> 01:32:48.530
You don't-- there's a lot
of darkness in here, right?

01:32:48.530 --> 01:32:50.660
That's the old film noir style.

01:32:50.660 --> 01:32:53.510
So it's deliberately
drawing on the earlier film

01:32:53.510 --> 01:32:57.400
noir style of the late
'40s or early '50s,

01:32:57.400 --> 01:33:03.467
that's right chronologically,
darkness, in shadow, to convey

01:33:03.467 --> 01:33:05.300
that sense to audiences
that would have been

01:33:05.300 --> 01:33:06.590
very familiar with that genre.

01:33:06.590 --> 01:33:09.410
The sense that, ah, this is some
dark mysterious detective story

01:33:09.410 --> 01:33:12.290
that we're going to
be taking you on.

01:33:12.290 --> 01:33:14.900
So one question is about the
kinds of aesthetic choices

01:33:14.900 --> 01:33:16.817
that they made to convey
the kind of narrative

01:33:16.817 --> 01:33:18.525
that they wanted to
convey about science,

01:33:18.525 --> 01:33:19.860
that this is a detective story.

01:33:19.860 --> 01:33:20.870
So that's one question.

01:33:20.870 --> 01:33:24.830
And then the other thing, as
Chris was just describing,

01:33:24.830 --> 01:33:27.230
the relationship between
the different episodes,

01:33:27.230 --> 01:33:30.870
it gets to this
question about accuracy.

01:33:30.870 --> 01:33:31.920
I mean so this--

01:33:31.920 --> 01:33:33.600
so the way they have doing this.

01:33:33.600 --> 01:33:37.070
Well, so you start out with
this transformation here

01:33:37.070 --> 01:33:40.350
and it has these incredible
repercussions way over here.

01:33:40.350 --> 01:33:44.060
And then one of the questions
that we kind of always

01:33:44.060 --> 01:33:46.580
have about the series, is
how much just it ended up

01:33:46.580 --> 01:33:49.700
being just so stories, and I
mean can you really kind of do

01:33:49.700 --> 01:33:54.685
that kind of linking in
quite the way that it does.

01:33:54.685 --> 01:33:56.810
CHRIS BOEBEL: They're all
very cool and interesting

01:33:56.810 --> 01:33:59.273
connections, but the
question is, at what point

01:33:59.273 --> 01:34:01.190
are they serving a
narrative and at what point

01:34:01.190 --> 01:34:04.085
are they really interest--

01:34:04.085 --> 01:34:06.710
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Not really in
any kind of logical connection,

01:34:06.710 --> 01:34:08.335
but they're just
asserting a connection

01:34:08.335 --> 01:34:10.490
because it's good thematically.

01:34:10.490 --> 01:34:12.230
Or narratively speaking.

01:34:12.230 --> 01:34:15.750
CHRIS BOEBEL: Let's
take a quick break.

01:34:15.750 --> 01:34:19.800
So we have the entire
series of this in the lab,

01:34:19.800 --> 01:34:20.950
if anybody's interested.

01:34:20.950 --> 01:34:25.385
I mean they do make these
very compelling stories

01:34:25.385 --> 01:34:27.510
and whether they're just
so stories, as Chris said,

01:34:27.510 --> 01:34:33.030
or whether they actually
have something legit to say

01:34:33.030 --> 01:34:36.450
about how one technology
feeds another.

01:34:36.450 --> 01:34:37.860
It's probably a bit of both.

01:34:37.860 --> 01:34:40.940
It's probably kind of a
mix of narrative flourish

01:34:40.940 --> 01:34:43.352
and substance.

01:34:43.352 --> 01:34:45.560
CHRISTINE WALLEY: But
certainly they're fun to watch,

01:34:45.560 --> 01:34:49.732
and they were, as we mentioned,
very influential in getting

01:34:49.732 --> 01:34:52.190
certain people to be interested
in these kinds of questions

01:34:52.190 --> 01:34:53.180
and want to explore them

01:34:53.180 --> 01:34:54.763
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well,
and I think in how

01:34:54.763 --> 01:34:57.230
people think about
science and technology,

01:34:57.230 --> 01:35:00.560
and in a kind of an
evolutionary way.

01:35:02.823 --> 01:35:05.240
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So at this
point any final comments on--

01:35:08.660 --> 01:35:13.448
any final comments
on Connections?

01:35:13.448 --> 01:35:14.990
AUDIENCE: I think
it's just funny how

01:35:14.990 --> 01:35:16.870
whenever there's
a mystery movie,

01:35:16.870 --> 01:35:19.480
it's they always like it has
to be like someone British

01:35:19.480 --> 01:35:21.070
or with an accent.

01:35:21.070 --> 01:35:22.490
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Right, exactly.

01:35:22.490 --> 01:35:22.990
Exactly.

01:35:22.990 --> 01:35:27.757
AUDIENCE: Any of like dark
and maybe like damp sort of.

01:35:27.757 --> 01:35:29.090
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, exactly.

01:35:29.090 --> 01:35:31.090
And what's funny, one of
the things that I think

01:35:31.090 --> 01:35:33.460
is absolutely hysterical,
well, he has an overcoat on,

01:35:33.460 --> 01:35:35.127
so you don't see it
a lot, but he always

01:35:35.127 --> 01:35:36.430
wears this tan leisure suit.

01:35:36.430 --> 01:35:37.630
And so you go through
all the episodes,

01:35:37.630 --> 01:35:39.070
he's always got the
tan leisure suit on,

01:35:39.070 --> 01:35:40.612
so it's like that's
one of the things

01:35:40.612 --> 01:35:43.090
that they use as an
element of continuity

01:35:43.090 --> 01:35:46.060
throughout a lot of the videos.

01:35:46.060 --> 01:35:51.460
But as you say, the British
accent, the kind of collar

01:35:51.460 --> 01:35:54.580
turned up, the
film noir lighting.

01:35:54.580 --> 01:35:58.720
So again, through various
kinds of aesthetic choices,

01:35:58.720 --> 01:36:00.790
and how people are dressed,
what kinds of things

01:36:00.790 --> 01:36:04.450
get conveyed when you go to
make your final projects?

01:36:04.450 --> 01:36:08.122
How do those kinds
of aesthetic elements

01:36:08.122 --> 01:36:09.580
affect the kind of
narrative that's

01:36:09.580 --> 01:36:10.705
going to end up being told?

01:36:13.290 --> 01:36:18.010
OK, so just to summarize
some of this up.

01:36:18.010 --> 01:36:20.850
So one of the things
we've been wanting

01:36:20.850 --> 01:36:23.700
to think about here
is the question,

01:36:23.700 --> 01:36:26.310
or at least get out on the
table, what kinds of narratives

01:36:26.310 --> 01:36:29.340
have commonly been
told about science

01:36:29.340 --> 01:36:30.900
through documentary film?

01:36:30.900 --> 01:36:33.113
And there's been certain
genres of documentary

01:36:33.113 --> 01:36:34.530
filmmaking about
science that have

01:36:34.530 --> 01:36:35.740
been more common than others.

01:36:35.740 --> 01:36:37.380
For example, this kind
of detective version,

01:36:37.380 --> 01:36:39.330
or the educational
film, or whatever it is.

01:36:39.330 --> 01:36:41.663
But one of the other questions
that we want to throw out

01:36:41.663 --> 01:36:44.190
is, what kinds of
narratives about science

01:36:44.190 --> 01:36:46.410
have not been commonly told?

01:36:46.410 --> 01:36:47.140
Right?

01:36:47.140 --> 01:36:49.740
And I think there's a lot of
other narratives out there

01:36:49.740 --> 01:36:52.260
about science and technology
that haven't necessarily

01:36:52.260 --> 01:36:54.290
been told a whole lot.

01:36:54.290 --> 01:36:58.637
And so Chris and I, when we
were thinking about setting up

01:36:58.637 --> 01:37:00.720
this class, I mean one of
the things that we tried

01:37:00.720 --> 01:37:02.160
to think about was
the direct cinema

01:37:02.160 --> 01:37:04.030
style which we're going to talk
about in a couple of weeks.

01:37:04.030 --> 01:37:06.360
But if you think again back
to that film primary where

01:37:06.360 --> 01:37:08.400
it's like hand-held
cameras, focusing

01:37:08.400 --> 01:37:10.200
on the action moving around.

01:37:10.200 --> 01:37:13.020
It's much rarer,
for example, to see

01:37:13.020 --> 01:37:15.300
films made about science
and technology that

01:37:15.300 --> 01:37:18.573
draw upon those kinds
of techniques compared

01:37:18.573 --> 01:37:20.490
to some others, or certain
kinds of narratives

01:37:20.490 --> 01:37:23.260
that would come out of that
kind of style of filmmaking.

01:37:23.260 --> 01:37:25.200
And so one of the
things that we want

01:37:25.200 --> 01:37:29.040
you to be thinking about
through the course of this class

01:37:29.040 --> 01:37:32.430
is, OK, so there's the
more common narratives that

01:37:32.430 --> 01:37:35.760
get told about science, and
those might be very compelling,

01:37:35.760 --> 01:37:37.530
and you might very
well want to draw

01:37:37.530 --> 01:37:40.968
a final project that draws on
some of those kinds of genres.

01:37:40.968 --> 01:37:42.510
Or you might also
want to be thinking

01:37:42.510 --> 01:37:44.218
about what kinds of
stories about science

01:37:44.218 --> 01:37:47.080
haven't been told as much
that maybe I want to tell.

01:37:47.080 --> 01:37:47.580
OK?

01:37:47.580 --> 01:37:49.560
So we want to keep
it all open to you,

01:37:49.560 --> 01:37:51.330
and get you thinking
about all of those.

01:37:51.330 --> 01:37:53.550
And the way we tried
to set up the class

01:37:53.550 --> 01:37:56.343
is that throughout
the course, you're

01:37:56.343 --> 01:37:58.260
going to be exposed to
lots of different kinds

01:37:58.260 --> 01:38:01.530
of documentary
filmmaking techniques,

01:38:01.530 --> 01:38:03.960
as well as a whole range of
different kinds of narrative

01:38:03.960 --> 01:38:07.140
styles for talking about
science and documentary.

01:38:07.140 --> 01:38:10.933
So the idea is to give
you a broad toolkit

01:38:10.933 --> 01:38:12.600
both in terms of the
kinds of narratives

01:38:12.600 --> 01:38:15.840
you might want to use, or
you might want to be telling,

01:38:15.840 --> 01:38:17.790
but also in terms of
the kinds of techniques

01:38:17.790 --> 01:38:20.770
or styles of shooting that
you might want to engage in.

01:38:20.770 --> 01:38:22.650
So the idea is to give
you this-- present

01:38:22.650 --> 01:38:25.810
this whole range of things.

01:38:25.810 --> 01:38:27.510
And then this is your toolbox.

01:38:27.510 --> 01:38:30.690
And you get to decide what kind
of story do you want to tell,

01:38:30.690 --> 01:38:34.380
and it order to tell that
story, what kinds of tools

01:38:34.380 --> 01:38:35.670
do you want to use?

01:38:35.670 --> 01:38:37.950
What kind of narrative
do you want to convey?

01:38:37.950 --> 01:38:42.900
What kind of narrative best gets
at what you want to get across?

01:38:42.900 --> 01:38:43.620
OK?

01:38:43.620 --> 01:38:46.920
So that is the idea for that.

01:38:46.920 --> 01:38:48.630
Anything to add onto that?

01:38:48.630 --> 01:38:50.880
CHRIS BOEBEL: No, I think
that's a good summary.

01:38:50.880 --> 01:38:51.755
CHRISTINE WALLEY: OK.

01:38:51.755 --> 01:38:54.840
So then before we end
then the last little thing

01:38:54.840 --> 01:38:56.697
that we want to do
is just talk a bit

01:38:56.697 --> 01:38:58.530
more about your final
project so that you're

01:38:58.530 --> 01:39:00.870
thinking more about that.

01:39:00.870 --> 01:39:05.010
Again, I know-- so Sabrina
and Jessica and Katrina,

01:39:05.010 --> 01:39:07.860
and both of you, you were also
at the DV lab screening, right?

01:39:07.860 --> 01:39:09.330
At the museum?

01:39:09.330 --> 01:39:15.060
So I don't if there was any
reaction to those things

01:39:15.060 --> 01:39:16.800
that you thought
were interesting

01:39:16.800 --> 01:39:19.278
as topics or narratives or
things to potentially explore,

01:39:19.278 --> 01:39:20.820
or things that you
might have thought

01:39:20.820 --> 01:39:23.340
would have been interesting
kinds of projects

01:39:23.340 --> 01:39:24.870
that weren't necessarily--

01:39:24.870 --> 01:39:28.810
didn't necessarily be made.

01:39:28.810 --> 01:39:31.560
Anyway, any kind of responses
to the ones that you saw,

01:39:31.560 --> 01:39:33.425
or if you just want
to skip to what

01:39:33.425 --> 01:39:35.050
you're potentially
interested in doing.

01:39:35.050 --> 01:39:37.320
I don't know how much thought
you've put into what you do.

01:39:37.320 --> 01:39:38.940
CHRIS BOEBEL: Obviously this
is very casual at this point.

01:39:38.940 --> 01:39:40.440
There is kind of
a formal process

01:39:40.440 --> 01:39:45.240
later in the class of people
presenting ideas, and being

01:39:45.240 --> 01:39:47.400
able to pick between
several ideas,

01:39:47.400 --> 01:39:49.810
hopefully, so that you
don't have to settle very,

01:39:49.810 --> 01:39:51.000
very quickly on something.

01:39:51.000 --> 01:39:51.600
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Right.

01:39:51.600 --> 01:39:53.190
So right at this
stage now we just

01:39:53.190 --> 01:39:56.710
want preliminary just to get you
starting to think about stuff.

01:39:56.710 --> 01:39:59.550
And even if you have the
vaguest idea or maybe you

01:39:59.550 --> 01:40:00.980
don't even have any idea.

01:40:00.980 --> 01:40:01.980
I mean any of that's OK.

01:40:01.980 --> 01:40:06.625
But I'm just wondering
what you're potentially

01:40:06.625 --> 01:40:07.500
thinking about doing.

01:40:07.500 --> 01:40:09.060
And something else
to think about

01:40:09.060 --> 01:40:11.650
is, again, this is a class--

01:40:11.650 --> 01:40:13.180
it's not film school.

01:40:13.180 --> 01:40:13.680
Right?

01:40:13.680 --> 01:40:15.490
You don't have massive
amounts of time.

01:40:15.490 --> 01:40:17.240
Also, you have to be
thinking about if you

01:40:17.240 --> 01:40:21.330
want to tell a particular
story, do you have enough time

01:40:21.330 --> 01:40:22.870
to be able to tell that story?

01:40:22.870 --> 01:40:23.370
Right?

01:40:23.370 --> 01:40:26.190
So we're not asking you to
do these massive projects.

01:40:26.190 --> 01:40:29.100
And keep in mind again that
the DV lab videos that you saw,

01:40:29.100 --> 01:40:30.790
students worked on
it one semester.

01:40:30.790 --> 01:40:32.670
But then they also had
a half class follow up

01:40:32.670 --> 01:40:34.260
where they just worked
on whatever project

01:40:34.260 --> 01:40:35.280
that they were doing.

01:40:35.280 --> 01:40:37.160
CHRIS BOEBEL: Most of
them came out of that.

01:40:37.160 --> 01:40:38.868
CHRISTINE WALLEY: A
few of them were just

01:40:38.868 --> 01:40:42.340
from the first semester.

01:40:42.340 --> 01:40:43.860
But for your final
project, so we're

01:40:43.860 --> 01:40:45.870
not expecting some
half hour piece.

01:40:45.870 --> 01:40:49.350
In fact, we would encourage
people to keep it small.

01:40:49.350 --> 01:40:50.580
How would you?

01:40:50.580 --> 01:40:54.660
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, time wise
certainly probably under 10

01:40:54.660 --> 01:40:56.280
minutes, realistically.

01:40:56.280 --> 01:40:57.450
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Somewhere
between two and 10 minutes.

01:40:57.450 --> 01:40:59.860
CHRIS BOEBEL: Some the ones
we saw on Friday were longer.

01:40:59.860 --> 01:41:04.360
But again, they had
longer to work on them.

01:41:04.360 --> 01:41:06.100
AUDIENCE: It was
interesting to see

01:41:06.100 --> 01:41:10.720
with the contrast between
Tom's piece and Emily's piece,

01:41:10.720 --> 01:41:13.360
and I was trying to see
where I want situate myself

01:41:13.360 --> 01:41:14.290
along this continuum.

01:41:14.290 --> 01:41:18.490
Just go somewhere and
shoot everything and trying

01:41:18.490 --> 01:41:22.030
to get to shoot interesting
things and people saying things

01:41:22.030 --> 01:41:28.810
that have some kind of powerful
narrative input in themselves.

01:41:28.810 --> 01:41:30.730
And then getting
lots of material

01:41:30.730 --> 01:41:34.990
is also kind of the
point and, then you

01:41:34.990 --> 01:41:37.040
have this incredible
editing problem.

01:41:37.040 --> 01:41:39.757
And I think Tom went a
completely different process

01:41:39.757 --> 01:41:41.840
also for the nature of the
questions he was asking

01:41:41.840 --> 01:41:44.380
and started going through
[? tell ?] and to completely

01:41:44.380 --> 01:41:48.610
construct everything, and
basically have actors.

01:41:48.610 --> 01:41:51.670
[INAUDIBLE] the controversy,
so you had the guy on Skype.

01:41:51.670 --> 01:41:54.400
Then you had PhD student
talking about the lab, and--

01:41:54.400 --> 01:41:56.680
CHRISTINE WALLEY: The
electron microscopy lab,

01:41:56.680 --> 01:41:57.790
with the conflict within.

01:41:57.790 --> 01:42:00.040
And Emily's piece at the
end, hers was one about.

01:42:00.040 --> 01:42:05.140
Yeah, the cows, the
agricultural fair.

01:42:05.140 --> 01:42:07.060
So and [? Ty, ?]
you weren't there,

01:42:07.060 --> 01:42:10.210
but so the first
piece is much more

01:42:10.210 --> 01:42:13.060
interview based talking
about this controversy

01:42:13.060 --> 01:42:13.907
within the lab.

01:42:13.907 --> 01:42:16.240
And we can raise there a
certain-- the kind of question,

01:42:16.240 --> 01:42:19.042
too, about what kinds of things
can you get access to or not.

01:42:19.042 --> 01:42:20.500
And he ended up
having reenactments

01:42:20.500 --> 01:42:23.410
because he couldn't get access
to certain kinds of things,

01:42:23.410 --> 01:42:25.640
which we should talk about.

01:42:25.640 --> 01:42:30.820
And then Emily's piece, much
more observational style,

01:42:30.820 --> 01:42:33.790
where it's more quietly
going around with the camera

01:42:33.790 --> 01:42:35.975
and just watching
what people are doing.

01:42:35.975 --> 01:42:37.600
CHRIS BOEBEL: There
is a big-- actually

01:42:37.600 --> 01:42:42.508
a strong similarity to the
two pieces in the origination,

01:42:42.508 --> 01:42:44.800
however, because both of them
started out with an idea,

01:42:44.800 --> 01:42:48.250
with an abstract
idea, not a story,

01:42:48.250 --> 01:42:50.920
or a profile of a person.

01:42:50.920 --> 01:42:56.650
Tom wanted to talk about
some fairly abstract things

01:42:56.650 --> 01:42:59.350
about imaging, and
what images mean,

01:42:59.350 --> 01:43:01.480
and what the relationship
between something

01:43:01.480 --> 01:43:04.390
you see and evidence.

01:43:04.390 --> 01:43:07.300
And also to start to take a
look at this controversy which

01:43:07.300 --> 01:43:09.250
had happened in the
past, the people

01:43:09.250 --> 01:43:11.590
had left MIT, except
for one person.

01:43:11.590 --> 01:43:15.400
He had no material on it,
no footage or anything.

01:43:15.400 --> 01:43:20.320
So he had to find a story
to put all of that stuff on.

01:43:20.320 --> 01:43:23.140
And create it as he went along.

01:43:23.140 --> 01:43:26.380
And Emily basically went
to the fair with this idea

01:43:26.380 --> 01:43:30.520
that she wanted to shoot
things that involved animal

01:43:30.520 --> 01:43:34.300
human interactions,
and she had to find

01:43:34.300 --> 01:43:36.580
a story in the edit room.

01:43:36.580 --> 01:43:40.390
She just shot for three or four
days, shot a lot of material,

01:43:40.390 --> 01:43:43.018
and found a really nice
interesting narrative

01:43:43.018 --> 01:43:44.560
with all of these
kind of connections

01:43:44.560 --> 01:43:47.680
that get made through the film.

01:43:47.680 --> 01:43:50.110
So again, the idea
in both situations

01:43:50.110 --> 01:43:54.520
was that they didn't have a
strong narrative like a process

01:43:54.520 --> 01:43:59.200
or a profile of a
person or a competition.

01:43:59.200 --> 01:44:02.170
A lot of the things that
we were just talking about,

01:44:02.170 --> 01:44:05.948
they had to find those
things as they went along.

01:44:05.948 --> 01:44:08.240
CHRISTINE WALLEY: What Chris
means is that some of this

01:44:08.240 --> 01:44:11.810
like detective story,
it has intrinsic to it

01:44:11.810 --> 01:44:13.520
a certain kind of narrative arc.

01:44:13.520 --> 01:44:15.530
But if you don't enter--
or a competition,

01:44:15.530 --> 01:44:16.820
if you film a competition.

01:44:16.820 --> 01:44:19.370
That has its own kind of
narrative arc built into it.

01:44:19.370 --> 01:44:21.320
But if you're
filming a topic that

01:44:21.320 --> 01:44:25.280
doesn't have a kind of
drama that's automatically

01:44:25.280 --> 01:44:27.140
built into the
topic, then you have

01:44:27.140 --> 01:44:29.948
to create the narrative thread.

01:44:29.948 --> 01:44:32.240
And so you do it through
editing or through other kinds

01:44:32.240 --> 01:44:32.840
of means.

01:44:32.840 --> 01:44:34.382
CHRIS BOEBEL: An
example of something

01:44:34.382 --> 01:44:37.310
that has a built in
narrative arc, last year

01:44:37.310 --> 01:44:40.700
one of the students for an
earlier project shot a very

01:44:40.700 --> 01:44:45.200
interesting fun little film
about the pie eating contest

01:44:45.200 --> 01:44:46.730
at Pi Day.

01:44:46.730 --> 01:44:48.320
And it was great.

01:44:48.320 --> 01:44:51.650
People sit down, who's
going to win, pies.

01:44:51.650 --> 01:44:52.160
It's fun.

01:44:52.160 --> 01:44:54.800
There's all sorts
of interesting--

01:44:54.800 --> 01:44:58.970
oh no, not the pie eating
contest, the naming the numbers

01:44:58.970 --> 01:45:00.050
of pi.

01:45:00.050 --> 01:45:02.660
How many places of pi you name.

01:45:02.660 --> 01:45:06.180
And there's pie
at the end of it.

01:45:06.180 --> 01:45:09.200
So but it's a
competition, there are

01:45:09.200 --> 01:45:11.330
interesting quirky
characters and moments.

01:45:11.330 --> 01:45:13.340
You get kind of involved
in who's going to win.

01:45:13.340 --> 01:45:14.460
Somebody wins.

01:45:14.460 --> 01:45:16.070
And there's a denouement.

01:45:16.070 --> 01:45:18.330
So there's a narrative
built into the story.

01:45:18.330 --> 01:45:22.940
And she went in knowing what
the narrative was going to be.

01:45:22.940 --> 01:45:26.090
Right, and somebody also
did a very fun piece

01:45:26.090 --> 01:45:29.575
on student elections,
running for office.

01:45:29.575 --> 01:45:31.700
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So and
then somebody wins, right?

01:45:31.700 --> 01:45:33.030
So again, there's closure to it.

01:45:33.030 --> 01:45:34.530
So there are certain
kinds of topics

01:45:34.530 --> 01:45:36.830
you can pick that are
easier in some sense

01:45:36.830 --> 01:45:39.050
because they have
built into them, again,

01:45:39.050 --> 01:45:40.550
a certain kind of drama.

01:45:40.550 --> 01:45:43.045
And if you decide you
want to do a topic that

01:45:43.045 --> 01:45:45.170
might be incredibly
fascinating and wonderful to do

01:45:45.170 --> 01:45:46.760
that doesn't have
that, again, it just

01:45:46.760 --> 01:45:48.590
means you just have to do
some additional kind of work

01:45:48.590 --> 01:45:50.180
in terms of thinking
about how you're

01:45:50.180 --> 01:45:52.340
going to draw out or
create some kind of story.

01:45:52.340 --> 01:45:55.670
Because ultimately,
in order for anybody

01:45:55.670 --> 01:45:58.760
to want to watch something, you
have to have some kind of story

01:45:58.760 --> 01:45:59.330
be there.

01:45:59.330 --> 01:46:02.460
You have to be saying something
with what it is that you shoot.

01:46:02.460 --> 01:46:02.960
OK?

01:46:02.960 --> 01:46:04.070
And maybe that at the beginning.

01:46:04.070 --> 01:46:05.660
Maybe not when you start
the shooting process.

01:46:05.660 --> 01:46:06.860
I mean again, people
come to these things

01:46:06.860 --> 01:46:08.440
in very different kinds of ways.

01:46:08.440 --> 01:46:10.950
A kind of a very anthropological
approach to shooting

01:46:10.950 --> 01:46:12.950
would be, OK, well, I'm
interested in this topic

01:46:12.950 --> 01:46:15.377
kind of like Emily with
the agricultural fair.

01:46:15.377 --> 01:46:17.210
I'm interested in animal
human interactions,

01:46:17.210 --> 01:46:19.482
but I'm not going
to assume anything

01:46:19.482 --> 01:46:20.690
about what I'm going to find.

01:46:20.690 --> 01:46:21.860
I'm just going to go and shoot.

01:46:21.860 --> 01:46:23.560
And I'm gonna see, and I'm
going to think about it,

01:46:23.560 --> 01:46:25.018
and then I've got
all this footage,

01:46:25.018 --> 01:46:26.490
and I'm going to ponder that.

01:46:26.490 --> 01:46:27.660
Well, what was that about?

01:46:27.660 --> 01:46:29.810
And then through the
process of pondering that,

01:46:29.810 --> 01:46:34.920
you decide like, oh, this is
kind of what this is about.

01:46:34.920 --> 01:46:37.610
And you start to put together
the visual images in a way

01:46:37.610 --> 01:46:39.420
that kind of tell that story.

01:46:39.420 --> 01:46:43.550
So some people have it more kind
of as a process of exploration

01:46:43.550 --> 01:46:45.230
in the world, and
you pull it out.

01:46:45.230 --> 01:46:47.810
And others kind of have
something either more

01:46:47.810 --> 01:46:51.020
built into the event itself, or
people have some kind of story

01:46:51.020 --> 01:46:52.520
that they want to
tell more clearly,

01:46:52.520 --> 01:46:54.062
and they're looking
to illustrate it.

01:46:55.693 --> 01:46:57.360
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
then the other thing I

01:46:57.360 --> 01:46:59.100
think we both
encourage you to think

01:46:59.100 --> 01:47:03.870
about are stories of
Science and Technology

01:47:03.870 --> 01:47:05.010
that are nontraditional.

01:47:05.010 --> 01:47:07.920
I mean, there are
the lab stories.

01:47:07.920 --> 01:47:12.330
But we define science and
technology pretty broadly.

01:47:12.330 --> 01:47:14.940
In fact, really broadly.

01:47:14.940 --> 01:47:17.460
The puppet theater
is not exactly--

01:47:17.460 --> 01:47:22.170
I mean there's, again, a craft
portion to what they're doing,

01:47:22.170 --> 01:47:25.860
craft technology, but it's a
pretty broad interpretation

01:47:25.860 --> 01:47:29.070
of what a science or technology
related film would be.

01:47:29.070 --> 01:47:31.530
And there are a lot of really
interesting things going

01:47:31.530 --> 01:47:34.998
on at MIT right now that
we would both encourage

01:47:34.998 --> 01:47:35.790
you to think about.

01:47:35.790 --> 01:47:37.998
There's the Festival of Art,
Science, and Technology,

01:47:37.998 --> 01:47:44.280
which is just filled with really
interesting events and projects

01:47:44.280 --> 01:47:49.230
and happenings between now
and the end of the semester.

01:47:49.230 --> 01:47:54.270
There are multiple
possibilities there for a film.

01:47:54.270 --> 01:47:57.630
Which isn't to say
that a fantastic

01:47:57.630 --> 01:48:01.975
film about lab research is
not a great way to go too.

01:48:02.475 --> 01:48:04.860
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Which
could really terrific.

01:48:04.860 --> 01:48:07.200
There's also the 150th
is going on now too,

01:48:07.200 --> 01:48:13.700
so you could do a film about MIT
reflecting on itself at 150th.

01:48:13.700 --> 01:48:15.090
You know so it
doesn't have to be

01:48:15.090 --> 01:48:17.100
MIT'S narrative
about itself, but it

01:48:17.100 --> 01:48:19.553
could be your take on what
they're presenting is that.

01:48:19.553 --> 01:48:21.470
I'm just trying to throw
out other things that

01:48:21.470 --> 01:48:23.803
are out there going around
that are happening right now.

01:48:27.442 --> 01:48:28.900
Things that are
happening right now

01:48:28.900 --> 01:48:30.520
are obviously easier to shoot.

01:48:30.520 --> 01:48:32.830
I mean, we did the
film that Christa--

01:48:32.830 --> 01:48:34.687
that I helped out on, Chris--

01:48:34.687 --> 01:48:36.520
I think I mentioned
this in the first class,

01:48:36.520 --> 01:48:38.230
he did a film about the 19--

01:48:38.230 --> 01:48:41.980
documentary about the
1979 nuclear accident

01:48:41.980 --> 01:48:42.970
at Three Mile Island.

01:48:42.970 --> 01:48:47.830
So we were making the film
much after the accident.

01:48:47.830 --> 01:48:50.080
So there's interviews with
all the people in the area.

01:48:50.080 --> 01:48:53.350
But there's also a strong
reliance on archival footage,

01:48:53.350 --> 01:48:55.160
and getting people to
talk about the past.

01:48:55.160 --> 01:48:57.493
And so the film becomes very
much about memory, as well.

01:48:57.493 --> 01:48:59.518
So if you want to get
at a historical topic,

01:48:59.518 --> 01:49:01.060
there's a whole
other range of issues

01:49:01.060 --> 01:49:03.953
that you have to deal with if
it's stuff that's not happening

01:49:03.953 --> 01:49:04.870
in the current moment.

01:49:04.870 --> 01:49:06.460
How do you tell that story?

01:49:06.460 --> 01:49:09.850
If it's something that's
happening right now,

01:49:09.850 --> 01:49:12.370
I mean, that's something
that's really well-suited

01:49:12.370 --> 01:49:14.020
to say the direct
cinema style, which

01:49:14.020 --> 01:49:16.780
is a very kind of observational
style where just go out

01:49:16.780 --> 01:49:19.010
and explore that
thing that's going on.

01:49:19.010 --> 01:49:21.310
But if you want to do
something about the past,

01:49:21.310 --> 01:49:22.540
you can do something
about Doc Edgerton.

01:49:22.540 --> 01:49:23.470
You can do something about--

01:49:23.470 --> 01:49:24.428
I mean, God knows what.

01:49:24.428 --> 01:49:26.560
I mean there's a zillion topics.

01:49:26.560 --> 01:49:30.670
CHRIS BOEBEL: And just, I think
as we've mentioned before,

01:49:30.670 --> 01:49:33.220
film tends to love action.

01:49:33.220 --> 01:49:34.000
It loves movement.

01:49:34.000 --> 01:49:35.740
It loves activity.

01:49:35.740 --> 01:49:39.940
Action films is not just what
we think of as action films.

01:49:39.940 --> 01:49:41.920
I mean, films are about actions.

01:49:41.920 --> 01:49:45.340
And that isn't to say that you
can't start with a very, very

01:49:45.340 --> 01:49:48.160
abstract idea, but you
just need to come up

01:49:48.160 --> 01:49:53.590
with a strategy for turning
that abstract idea into actions.

01:49:53.590 --> 01:49:57.340
And if you think about the
clip of Fast, Cheap, and Out

01:49:57.340 --> 01:50:03.130
of Control that we saw that has
this very, very abstract idea

01:50:03.130 --> 01:50:12.550
about groups and networks
and controlling nature,

01:50:12.550 --> 01:50:15.310
and Rodney Brooks,
it's brought alive

01:50:15.310 --> 01:50:18.370
with all of this a found
footage, archival footage,

01:50:18.370 --> 01:50:21.580
cartoons, all of this
stuff sore of montaged

01:50:21.580 --> 01:50:26.050
together, edited together
in a very interesting way.

01:50:26.050 --> 01:50:28.960
Emily had a very abstract
idea, but what was great

01:50:28.960 --> 01:50:30.580
was that she had
people doing stuff.

01:50:30.580 --> 01:50:34.690
Competing in various
competitions, this fair,

01:50:34.690 --> 01:50:36.070
it was incredibly visual.

01:50:36.070 --> 01:50:39.790
Movement, lots of
animals running around.

01:50:39.790 --> 01:50:43.540
So it became very easy for
her to turn that abstract idea

01:50:43.540 --> 01:50:47.290
into something that
was film friendly.

01:50:47.290 --> 01:50:50.140
Tom, of course,
had to use actors.

01:50:50.140 --> 01:50:53.200
That was his choice,
to make his--

01:50:53.200 --> 01:50:56.110
turn his abstract idea and his
thing that happened in the past

01:50:56.110 --> 01:50:56.877
into actions.

01:50:56.877 --> 01:50:59.210
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Well, and
there was the related issue,

01:50:59.210 --> 01:51:00.970
which we should at
least mention, too,

01:51:00.970 --> 01:51:03.600
that there's issue of access
and what you can get permission

01:51:03.600 --> 01:51:04.100
for.

01:51:04.100 --> 01:51:06.253
And again, there's
permission forms

01:51:06.253 --> 01:51:07.670
that when you go
to do interviews,

01:51:07.670 --> 01:51:10.430
you have to get permission
from people when you do this.

01:51:10.430 --> 01:51:13.300
And one of the issues about--

01:51:13.300 --> 01:51:15.580
I mean, we were strongly
encouraging people

01:51:15.580 --> 01:51:17.230
last semester, again,
to go out and try

01:51:17.230 --> 01:51:19.390
to explore new
narratives about science,

01:51:19.390 --> 01:51:21.880
and the idea of
doing a more kind

01:51:21.880 --> 01:51:24.940
of focusing on
science's process,

01:51:24.940 --> 01:51:27.070
and getting some
of the day to day

01:51:27.070 --> 01:51:29.140
of how science and
engineering actually plays out

01:51:29.140 --> 01:51:30.790
in a day to day way
is really fascinating.

01:51:30.790 --> 01:51:32.540
Something that hasn't
been done that much.

01:51:32.540 --> 01:51:35.230
But there's also, if you choose
to go that route, which I think

01:51:35.230 --> 01:51:37.750
is a really fabulous route to
go, and I'd encourage you--

01:51:37.750 --> 01:51:41.235
I mean that's one route that
could be really cool to do.

01:51:41.235 --> 01:51:43.360
But one of the difficulties
with that that students

01:51:43.360 --> 01:51:45.370
last semester found
out was that you

01:51:45.370 --> 01:51:47.680
have to think about,
there might be

01:51:47.680 --> 01:51:50.060
a lot of issues around
permissions, for example.

01:51:50.060 --> 01:51:50.560
Right?

01:51:50.560 --> 01:51:52.810
So what would happen was
that a number of the students

01:51:52.810 --> 01:51:55.450
last semester, they found
some totally cool thing

01:51:55.450 --> 01:51:57.530
that was happening in
some lab on campus.

01:51:57.530 --> 01:51:59.530
And they had friends who
were graduate students,

01:51:59.530 --> 01:52:01.510
the graduate student was
like, yeah, this is great,

01:52:01.510 --> 01:52:03.220
and maybe they did
preliminary interviews

01:52:03.220 --> 01:52:06.020
with that grad student,
and fantastic stuff.

01:52:06.020 --> 01:52:09.190
But then they had to ask the
PI of that graduate student

01:52:09.190 --> 01:52:11.680
and the PI said no.

01:52:11.680 --> 01:52:17.920
Because there's worry in
certain labs, understandably,

01:52:17.920 --> 01:52:22.450
that if stuff gets out
before it's been published,

01:52:22.450 --> 01:52:28.750
it's kind of a
pre-publication kind of--

01:52:28.750 --> 01:52:30.135
what's going on
inside the lab is

01:52:30.135 --> 01:52:31.510
going to be
disseminated in a way

01:52:31.510 --> 01:52:34.000
that they don't necessarily
want, if it hasn't necessarily

01:52:34.000 --> 01:52:34.960
been published yet.

01:52:34.960 --> 01:52:36.070
And then there's also--

01:52:36.070 --> 01:52:38.620
I mean, for obvious
reasons people

01:52:38.620 --> 01:52:40.790
like to control the
images of themselves.

01:52:40.790 --> 01:52:41.290
Right?

01:52:41.290 --> 01:52:45.880
So if you're the head of a lab,
and you've got funding sources,

01:52:45.880 --> 01:52:48.295
it's not surprising
that people want

01:52:48.295 --> 01:52:50.170
to control the image
that's going to come out

01:52:50.170 --> 01:52:54.468
of their laboratory, the same
way that at MIT in general

01:52:54.468 --> 01:52:57.010
there's sometimes a desire to
control what's the image of MIT

01:52:57.010 --> 01:52:58.900
that that's going to come out.

01:52:58.900 --> 01:53:00.882
So that doesn't mean
you can't get permission

01:53:00.882 --> 01:53:02.590
to do those kinds of
things, but it might

01:53:02.590 --> 01:53:03.933
take longer than you think.

01:53:03.933 --> 01:53:05.350
So that was one
of the things that

01:53:05.350 --> 01:53:06.985
stuck some of the students.

01:53:06.985 --> 01:53:08.110
CHRIS BOEBEL: Don't assume.

01:53:08.110 --> 01:53:09.330
CHRISTINE WALLEY: That
slowed them down a bit.

01:53:09.330 --> 01:53:11.080
CHRIS BOEBEL:
Think about who you

01:53:11.080 --> 01:53:14.380
need to give you the final
say that you can do a project,

01:53:14.380 --> 01:53:15.970
if it involves things like that.

01:53:15.970 --> 01:53:17.050
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
really explain to them

01:53:17.050 --> 01:53:18.508
what you're planning
on doing, what

01:53:18.508 --> 01:53:20.830
you're not planning on doing.

01:53:20.830 --> 01:53:23.230
We've been putting the ones--

01:53:23.230 --> 01:53:25.390
the class videos that
we've had permissions for,

01:53:25.390 --> 01:53:27.140
we've put them up online,
but you don't necessarily

01:53:27.140 --> 01:53:27.890
have to do that.

01:53:27.890 --> 01:53:30.320
If you want to just shoot it
as an exercise for yourself,

01:53:30.320 --> 01:53:31.862
you can tell them
that it's not going

01:53:31.862 --> 01:53:36.897
to go up anywhere beyond that,
and then we abide by that.

01:53:36.897 --> 01:53:39.230
CHRIS BOEBEL: Although we
would, for your final projects

01:53:39.230 --> 01:53:40.813
we would strongly
encourage you to try

01:53:40.813 --> 01:53:43.640
to get the necessary
permissions so that it

01:53:43.640 --> 01:53:44.990
can be published online.

01:53:44.990 --> 01:53:46.850
Because it's just
better for you.

01:53:46.850 --> 01:53:47.900
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
if you do a final project

01:53:47.900 --> 01:53:49.820
and you put a ton
of work into it.

01:53:49.820 --> 01:53:50.600
CHRIS BOEBEL: You want
to able to share it.

01:53:50.600 --> 01:53:51.080
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Yeah, you're going

01:53:51.080 --> 01:53:53.330
to be sad then at the end
you can't show it to anybody

01:53:53.330 --> 01:53:54.350
other than the class.

01:53:54.350 --> 01:53:56.600
CHRIS BOEBEL: But there are
also creative workarounds.

01:53:56.600 --> 01:53:59.160
I mean, for those of you
who were there Friday night.

01:53:59.160 --> 01:54:02.870
Max Dennis did the film
about the researcher slash

01:54:02.870 --> 01:54:06.530
stand up comic, and
basically he was not

01:54:06.530 --> 01:54:11.695
allowed to say what lab he
worked in, what exactly he did.

01:54:11.695 --> 01:54:14.070
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Because he
was feeding cocaine to rats.

01:54:14.070 --> 01:54:18.242
And so they didn't want-- the
lab didn't want that disgust.

01:54:18.242 --> 01:54:18.950
CHRIS BOEBEL: So.

01:54:18.950 --> 01:54:20.330
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Although
he had all this YouTube.

01:54:20.330 --> 01:54:21.955
CHRIS BOEBEL: Right,
so he discussed it

01:54:21.955 --> 01:54:24.420
without talking about exactly
what school he went to,

01:54:24.420 --> 01:54:27.950
which wasn't MIT,
what lab he worked in.

01:54:27.950 --> 01:54:30.077
Any of his co-workers,
any of that.

01:54:30.077 --> 01:54:31.160
All of that was out of it.

01:54:31.160 --> 01:54:32.720
CHRISTINE WALLEY: So it
was made a joke out of,

01:54:32.720 --> 01:54:33.920
which in a way that worked.

01:54:33.920 --> 01:54:35.878
So this is something
that's different for those

01:54:35.878 --> 01:54:37.940
of us who are used to doing--

01:54:37.940 --> 01:54:42.170
I mean, those of us who work in
the social sciences, the way we

01:54:42.170 --> 01:54:43.820
usually handle this
in our written work

01:54:43.820 --> 01:54:45.110
is that we use pseudonyms.

01:54:45.110 --> 01:54:47.300
That's how we give
protection to the people

01:54:47.300 --> 01:54:49.590
that we do our research
on, is we use pseudonyms.

01:54:49.590 --> 01:54:54.980
So that people have privacy and
there's not harmful outcomes

01:54:54.980 --> 01:54:55.910
to them in the end.

01:54:55.910 --> 01:54:58.430
But when you're doing video
work, you can't do that.

01:54:58.430 --> 01:55:00.410
So that imposes certain
kinds of constraints

01:55:00.410 --> 01:55:02.005
in terms of what
stories you can tell,

01:55:02.005 --> 01:55:03.380
or how you would
do it in the way

01:55:03.380 --> 01:55:04.880
that Tom dealt with
that, because he

01:55:04.880 --> 01:55:07.310
was dealing with
controversies within a lab.

01:55:07.310 --> 01:55:12.530
That were very-- they were--

01:55:12.530 --> 01:55:14.690
passions were strong
around those controversies,

01:55:14.690 --> 01:55:16.988
and it stood to affect
people's reputations.

01:55:16.988 --> 01:55:19.280
And it could have-- some of
the graduate students felt,

01:55:19.280 --> 01:55:20.930
like, well, it could have
affect their reputation.

01:55:20.930 --> 01:55:23.250
It would make it difficult for
them to get work afterwards.

01:55:23.250 --> 01:55:24.667
So the way that
he dealt with that

01:55:24.667 --> 01:55:28.400
is that he had his friends
re-enact those scenes,

01:55:28.400 --> 01:55:29.900
and it was-- and
all of his friends

01:55:29.900 --> 01:55:31.400
were students who
had worked in labs

01:55:31.400 --> 01:55:32.400
and experience
something different.

01:55:32.400 --> 01:55:34.108
So it was actually
kind of this fun thing

01:55:34.108 --> 01:55:37.610
where they got to act out-- like
the drama within their own lab

01:55:37.610 --> 01:55:38.120
groups.

01:55:38.120 --> 01:55:40.783
So it ended up being a
really fun kind of reflection

01:55:40.783 --> 01:55:42.200
on what they
actually do, but that

01:55:42.200 --> 01:55:44.580
was a really creative way
he got around that issue.

01:55:44.580 --> 01:55:45.955
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
the other thing

01:55:45.955 --> 01:55:49.695
is he embargoed the
film all of last year.

01:55:49.695 --> 01:55:51.320
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Until the last person

01:55:51.320 --> 01:55:52.790
left MIT who had been involved.

01:55:52.790 --> 01:55:54.680
CHRIS BOEBEL: And now
it's on the front page

01:55:54.680 --> 01:55:56.390
of Tech TV as of today.

01:55:56.390 --> 01:55:59.202
Because we can finally show it.

01:55:59.202 --> 01:56:00.410
Because everybody's left MIT.

01:56:00.410 --> 01:56:01.192
So.

01:56:01.192 --> 01:56:02.150
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:56:02.150 --> 01:56:03.942
Anyway, so we've done
a lot of talking now,

01:56:03.942 --> 01:56:05.540
but just any of you
have any ideas yet

01:56:05.540 --> 01:56:07.580
on what potentially
you might want to do,

01:56:07.580 --> 01:56:10.460
or whether something
might be feasible?

01:56:10.460 --> 01:56:12.213
AUDIENCE: After
seeing the videos,

01:56:12.213 --> 01:56:14.630
I realize like it would have
been really helpful according

01:56:14.630 --> 01:56:17.190
to a lot of them to work with
the another person, a partner.

01:56:17.190 --> 01:56:21.200
So Ty and I were thinking
of doing a film about ROTC.

01:56:21.200 --> 01:56:22.370
And like [INAUDIBLE].

01:56:22.370 --> 01:56:23.600
AUDIENCE: What's yours?

01:56:23.600 --> 01:56:29.450
AUDIENCE: ROTC is the Reserve
Officer Training Corps.

01:56:29.450 --> 01:56:32.750
And so like, it would show
how their students are not

01:56:32.750 --> 01:56:35.150
only like preparing
to be soldiers,

01:56:35.150 --> 01:56:37.820
but also their engineering
and like science

01:56:37.820 --> 01:56:40.705
like educational background,
and how they balance it.

01:56:40.705 --> 01:56:42.830
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah,
that's a fascinating topic.

01:56:42.830 --> 01:56:44.750
Just to clarify one
thing, everybody

01:56:44.750 --> 01:56:47.540
does have to do their own
film project at the end.

01:56:47.540 --> 01:56:50.870
But you can still work together.

01:56:50.870 --> 01:56:55.160
I mean, what Tom was
talking about was when--

01:56:55.160 --> 01:56:57.860
the idea is, and this is the way
commonly works in film school,

01:56:57.860 --> 01:56:59.943
is that when people are
working on their projects,

01:56:59.943 --> 01:57:03.140
you draw on your classmates
to help you to do sound,

01:57:03.140 --> 01:57:05.158
or to help while
you're interviewing,

01:57:05.158 --> 01:57:06.950
maybe somebody does
the camera, because you

01:57:06.950 --> 01:57:08.580
need to be able to
talk and interact.

01:57:08.580 --> 01:57:12.920
So part of the idea is that you
build a cohort as time goes on,

01:57:12.920 --> 01:57:15.650
and other people help you, and
then you help them in turn.

01:57:15.650 --> 01:57:18.640
And it really does help the
whole quality of the filmmaking

01:57:18.640 --> 01:57:19.140
process.

01:57:19.140 --> 01:57:22.400
So there is this kind of
collaborative collective aspect

01:57:22.400 --> 01:57:24.680
to making this stuff that
we would really encourage.

01:57:24.680 --> 01:57:27.170
CHRIS BOEBEL: So we would
encourage you to work in teams,

01:57:27.170 --> 01:57:28.647
but to do--

01:57:28.647 --> 01:57:30.230
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
So the outcome would

01:57:30.230 --> 01:57:32.150
have to be separate
to be graded,

01:57:32.150 --> 01:57:33.733
it would have to be
graded separately.

01:57:33.733 --> 01:57:34.620
But you can even do--

01:57:34.620 --> 01:57:35.120
I mean--

01:57:35.120 --> 01:57:36.740
CHRIS BOEBEL: If you do
two related projects.

01:57:36.740 --> 01:57:37.370
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah, exactly.

01:57:37.370 --> 01:57:39.953
I mean so you could do the same
topic, if you wanted, and have

01:57:39.953 --> 01:57:41.840
two different takes
on it, or two--

01:57:41.840 --> 01:57:45.110
but for two different
visual products at the end.

01:57:45.110 --> 01:57:48.180
CHRIS BOEBEL: So if you
both did films on ROTC

01:57:48.180 --> 01:57:51.230
and shared a lot of
the camera duties,

01:57:51.230 --> 01:57:53.288
you would still need to
edit your own projects.

01:57:53.288 --> 01:57:54.830
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
Because part of it,

01:57:54.830 --> 01:57:59.180
in terms of grading to
be able to that people

01:57:59.180 --> 01:58:04.490
got the basics of how
to shoot, how to edit.

01:58:04.490 --> 01:58:06.200
Because again, we
don't want to, as we

01:58:06.200 --> 01:58:07.783
said in the very
first class, we don't

01:58:07.783 --> 01:58:12.350
want to have to grade people
on just the final product

01:58:12.350 --> 01:58:15.177
and how aesthetically
good it is,

01:58:15.177 --> 01:58:16.760
because people may
be coming from very

01:58:16.760 --> 01:58:17.690
different backgrounds.

01:58:17.690 --> 01:58:19.940
But so we also want to do a
fair amount of the grading

01:58:19.940 --> 01:58:22.023
in terms of amount of
effort that people put into.

01:58:22.023 --> 01:58:23.480
And if it's done
collaboratively,

01:58:23.480 --> 01:58:27.623
we can't tell if one
person did 3/4 of the work,

01:58:27.623 --> 01:58:30.040
and one person did a quarter,
it's harder for us to judge.

01:58:30.040 --> 01:58:30.610
CHRIS BOEBEL: And
you could certainly

01:58:30.610 --> 01:58:32.200
shoot collaboratively,
and we would strongly

01:58:32.200 --> 01:58:33.492
encourage everybody to do that.

01:58:33.492 --> 01:58:35.080
CHRISTINE WALLEY: Yeah.

01:58:35.080 --> 01:58:37.080
CHRIS BOEBEL: Does that
make sense, or is that--

01:58:37.080 --> 01:58:38.740
OK.

01:58:38.740 --> 01:58:42.930
AUDIENCE: So how--

01:58:42.930 --> 01:58:46.370
I mean, let's say we
have a topic in mind,

01:58:46.370 --> 01:58:48.940
but we don't much about it.

01:58:48.940 --> 01:58:52.440
So we don't know what kind
of stories there are there.

01:58:52.440 --> 01:58:55.170
So what's the best way?

01:58:55.170 --> 01:58:57.845
Would you just go
and start filming?

01:58:57.845 --> 01:58:59.220
And like I realized
the other day

01:58:59.220 --> 01:59:01.890
we were filming just the
very basic kind of scene

01:59:01.890 --> 01:59:03.570
of like somebody
chopping things,

01:59:03.570 --> 01:59:05.700
then there was a story to it.

01:59:05.700 --> 01:59:07.560
I mean there was a
kind of relationship,

01:59:07.560 --> 01:59:11.250
very sensual relationship with
vegetables, which I wouldn't

01:59:11.250 --> 01:59:14.670
have thought about until
when I could hear the sounds,

01:59:14.670 --> 01:59:17.640
and the vegetables
being eaten, and like

01:59:17.640 --> 01:59:19.710
seems like the beauty of--

01:59:19.710 --> 01:59:22.410
and it really came out
only when I was looking

01:59:22.410 --> 01:59:24.040
at it through the camera.

01:59:24.040 --> 01:59:32.340
So or should you think about
it more before starting?

01:59:32.340 --> 01:59:35.220
CHRIS BOEBEL: Well, there's
an interesting answer

01:59:35.220 --> 01:59:38.400
which comes out of the
technology that we're using.

01:59:38.400 --> 01:59:43.230
The way to begin a project
like some of these projects

01:59:43.230 --> 01:59:47.760
are traditionally would
have been to go do some pre

01:59:47.760 --> 01:59:51.400
interviews, to do a site visit.

01:59:51.400 --> 01:59:53.400
If you're-- say you're
doing something on a lab.

01:59:53.400 --> 01:59:55.230
You go visit the lab
without a camera.

01:59:55.230 --> 01:59:57.780
You interview the researchers.

01:59:57.780 --> 01:59:59.370
You just get the
lay of the land.

01:59:59.370 --> 02:00:01.290
You start you look for stories.

02:00:01.290 --> 02:00:03.480
And the advantage of
that is that they see you

02:00:03.480 --> 02:00:05.580
as a human being before
they see you as a camera.

02:00:05.580 --> 02:00:07.320
And that's really,
really important

02:00:07.320 --> 02:00:11.452
because you make a human
connection before you jump up

02:00:11.452 --> 02:00:13.410
and take out the camera,
stick it in their face

02:00:13.410 --> 02:00:17.100
and say, be in my movie.

02:00:17.100 --> 02:00:19.902
But on the other hand, the
great thing about these cameras

02:00:19.902 --> 02:00:22.110
is that they're so small
that you can just throw them

02:00:22.110 --> 02:00:24.310
in your bag and bring them.

02:00:24.310 --> 02:00:27.720
And so what I often
do, and what I

02:00:27.720 --> 02:00:30.120
would think that you
might want to consider

02:00:30.120 --> 02:00:31.890
is do exactly what I just said.

02:00:31.890 --> 02:00:36.420
Set up a site visit
with no camera,

02:00:36.420 --> 02:00:38.640
go in and meet the
people, but if you

02:00:38.640 --> 02:00:41.370
have the camera in your bag
and you're sensitive enough

02:00:41.370 --> 02:00:44.670
and have your antenna out
enough about what's going on,

02:00:44.670 --> 02:00:47.520
at some point it may be OK
and just completely natural

02:00:47.520 --> 02:00:49.990
for you to take out the camera.

02:00:49.990 --> 02:00:52.512
And you obviously need
to ask before you do it.

02:00:52.512 --> 02:00:54.720
You need to make sure that
you've kind of established

02:00:54.720 --> 02:00:56.910
a rapport and that
everything is good,

02:00:56.910 --> 02:00:59.220
and they're enthusiastic
and interested.

02:00:59.220 --> 02:01:02.082
And you'll want to listen to
yourself and your own instincts

02:01:02.082 --> 02:01:03.540
about whether or
not want to shoot.

02:01:03.540 --> 02:01:05.670
Because as you said, you
start seeing things really

02:01:05.670 --> 02:01:07.000
differently through the camera.

02:01:07.000 --> 02:01:08.730
And you have ideas that you
wouldn't have necessarily

02:01:08.730 --> 02:01:10.438
had if you're just
talking to the people.

02:01:10.438 --> 02:01:13.590
And I've been on the other
side of that coin, where

02:01:13.590 --> 02:01:17.160
I'm on a site visit or some
kind of pre-production thing,

02:01:17.160 --> 02:01:20.330
and I realize, oh my God, why
didn't I bring the camera?

02:01:20.330 --> 02:01:22.080
There's some amazing
stuff happening here,

02:01:22.080 --> 02:01:23.880
why can't I shoot it?

02:01:23.880 --> 02:01:26.550
But you just have to be
aware that, if you walk in

02:01:26.550 --> 02:01:29.700
with a camera right off the bat,
on your shoulder ready to go,

02:01:29.700 --> 02:01:31.710
you may alienate the people.

02:01:31.710 --> 02:01:35.290
And it's kind of a rude way
of introducing yourself.

02:01:35.290 --> 02:01:37.740
So this gives you the
best of both worlds.

02:01:37.740 --> 02:01:42.017
Go make a site visit,
don't plan to film,

02:01:42.017 --> 02:01:43.350
but have the camera in your bag.

02:01:43.350 --> 02:01:47.130
And if the moment is right,
and the rapport is good,

02:01:47.130 --> 02:01:49.230
and something really
fascinating is happening,

02:01:49.230 --> 02:01:51.870
you can ask and film.

02:01:51.870 --> 02:01:57.030
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And kind of
on the even more extreme end

02:01:57.030 --> 02:02:00.382
from that, say people with
anthropological training who

02:02:00.382 --> 02:02:02.340
go to make documentary
films, sometimes they'll

02:02:02.340 --> 02:02:04.590
spend a year before they
even take out a camera.

02:02:04.590 --> 02:02:07.080
And depending on where you
are, and if what's going on

02:02:07.080 --> 02:02:09.450
is really socially
sensitive, that people

02:02:09.450 --> 02:02:12.090
will spend that time just
building relationships, knowing

02:02:12.090 --> 02:02:18.300
what's going on, developing that
sense of relationship and trust

02:02:18.300 --> 02:02:18.970
with people.

02:02:18.970 --> 02:02:22.042
So that when they actually
go and pull out the camera,

02:02:22.042 --> 02:02:24.000
it's a completely different
product in the end,

02:02:24.000 --> 02:02:25.833
because you can feel
the kind of interaction

02:02:25.833 --> 02:02:28.620
between the people who are
being filmed and the filmmaker,

02:02:28.620 --> 02:02:30.412
and it's completely
different than somebody

02:02:30.412 --> 02:02:33.405
who just came in for a couple
of days to shoot something.

02:02:33.405 --> 02:02:34.780
It's a totally
different product.

02:02:34.780 --> 02:02:38.333
And so I can try to remember
now what films we're showing you

02:02:38.333 --> 02:02:39.750
for the ethnographic
film section.

02:02:39.750 --> 02:02:41.615
But maybe I'll switch
it a bit and try

02:02:41.615 --> 02:02:43.740
to pull out some that convey
that kind of long term

02:02:43.740 --> 02:02:44.425
relationship.

02:02:44.425 --> 02:02:46.800
So in some cases, when people
have the time and the money

02:02:46.800 --> 02:02:48.570
and the ability to
do it, people will

02:02:48.570 --> 02:02:52.890
spend a year or so in advance
just building relationships

02:02:52.890 --> 02:02:54.690
before they ever start filming.

02:02:54.690 --> 02:02:57.420
So obviously you can't do that.

02:02:57.420 --> 02:02:59.295
But.

02:02:59.295 --> 02:03:01.170
CHRIS BOEBEL: I mean,
the other thing is to--

02:03:01.170 --> 02:03:02.130
CHRISTINE WALLEY:
But just to emphasize

02:03:02.130 --> 02:03:04.320
the importance of the
social relationships

02:03:04.320 --> 02:03:06.510
and establishing the
comfort level of the people

02:03:06.510 --> 02:03:08.100
that you're working
with is really crucial.

02:03:08.100 --> 02:03:09.308
CHRIS BOEBEL: That's the key.

02:03:09.308 --> 02:03:11.250
But I was going
to say, even there

02:03:11.250 --> 02:03:13.310
has been a shift I
think with video,

02:03:13.310 --> 02:03:15.060
because video is so
cheap, and the cameras

02:03:15.060 --> 02:03:19.410
are so small and so ubiquitous
that you still spend the year

02:03:19.410 --> 02:03:20.910
or more building
the relationships,

02:03:20.910 --> 02:03:22.590
but you can introduce
the camera sooner.

02:03:22.590 --> 02:03:26.280
And then the camera just kind
of disappears, eventually.

02:03:26.280 --> 02:03:29.220
So-- but it's the
same principle.

02:03:29.220 --> 02:03:33.030
You've got to be seen
as a human being,

02:03:33.030 --> 02:03:37.230
and have that human relationship
before you become a camera.

02:03:37.230 --> 02:03:40.500
And if you do it at the
right pace or the best

02:03:40.500 --> 02:03:43.530
pace you possibly can given
your time and your budget

02:03:43.530 --> 02:03:46.620
and your schedule,
the camera will

02:03:46.620 --> 02:03:47.787
be more likely to disappear.

02:03:47.787 --> 02:03:49.662
CHRISTINE WALLEY: And
this is why it's better

02:03:49.662 --> 02:03:52.170
often to have two people-- if
you do have I mean some of you

02:03:52.170 --> 02:03:54.210
might be doing sit down
interviews, and some of you

02:03:54.210 --> 02:03:55.470
might be doing
handheld where you

02:03:55.470 --> 02:03:56.940
want it to be about
action, about movement,

02:03:56.940 --> 02:03:58.815
and you don't want to
do sit down interviews.

02:03:58.815 --> 02:04:00.610
But if you do decide
you want to do--

02:04:00.610 --> 02:04:01.555
well, either way.

02:04:01.555 --> 02:04:02.430
Either way you do it.

02:04:02.430 --> 02:04:04.140
If you're going to
be talking to people

02:04:04.140 --> 02:04:07.260
and have them talking back
directly to the camera,

02:04:07.260 --> 02:04:08.940
with comfort level,
people are often

02:04:08.940 --> 02:04:11.678
much more comfortable
talking, again, to--

02:04:11.678 --> 02:04:13.470
I'd be much more
comfortable talking to you

02:04:13.470 --> 02:04:16.420
if you didn't have
a camera in my face.

02:04:16.420 --> 02:04:18.900
So this is, again, where it's
really helpful to team up.

02:04:18.900 --> 02:04:21.020
That if you have somebody
else going with you,

02:04:21.020 --> 02:04:25.140
maybe somebody else could
help do the camera work,

02:04:25.140 --> 02:04:29.280
and maybe Katrina's is filming
me, but I'm talking to you.

02:04:29.280 --> 02:04:31.357
And you're really
paying attention to me,

02:04:31.357 --> 02:04:33.190
because you're not
worrying about the camera

02:04:33.190 --> 02:04:35.378
and whether it's
exposed right, and all

02:04:35.378 --> 02:04:36.670
these other kinds of questions.

02:04:36.670 --> 02:04:38.700
And we can establish a
certain kind of relationship

02:04:38.700 --> 02:04:39.870
that somebody else can film.

02:04:39.870 --> 02:04:43.005
And it makes it a more
intimate interaction.

02:04:43.005 --> 02:04:44.880
So this is, again, where
it's helpful to have

02:04:44.880 --> 02:04:48.153
some-- potentially have
somebody else go with.

02:04:48.153 --> 02:04:50.070
So you as a filmmaker,
you might either choose

02:04:50.070 --> 02:04:51.210
that you would
rather do the filming

02:04:51.210 --> 02:04:53.850
and have the other person do
the interviewing, or vise versa.

02:04:53.850 --> 02:04:57.870
But it's often helpful to have
somebody just kind of do--

02:04:57.870 --> 02:05:00.660
be about the social
relationship part.

02:05:00.660 --> 02:05:04.712
And somebody else focusing on
the technical ends, like our--

02:05:04.712 --> 02:05:07.170
do we have, again, the exposure
right, and the focus right,

02:05:07.170 --> 02:05:11.310
and our wires all
tangled up in a mess.

02:05:11.310 --> 02:05:12.740
And the batteries.

02:05:12.740 --> 02:05:14.130
Have someone focusing on that.

02:05:14.130 --> 02:05:16.650
CHRIS BOEBEL: One just one
last thing on this topic.

02:05:16.650 --> 02:05:19.050
A lot of really interesting
work has come out

02:05:19.050 --> 02:05:21.960
of this class when people
have filmed their friends,

02:05:21.960 --> 02:05:24.690
and the reason for that is
that people are obviously very

02:05:24.690 --> 02:05:28.398
comfortable with each other.

02:05:28.398 --> 02:05:30.690
There's a lot of trust, if
you're filming your friends.

02:05:30.690 --> 02:05:33.120
So some great work has happened.

02:05:33.120 --> 02:05:35.610
The downside of that is that
you're not taking yourself out

02:05:35.610 --> 02:05:38.190
of a comfort zone
as a filmmaker.

02:05:38.190 --> 02:05:41.790
You're doing-- you're
shooting fish in a barrel.

02:05:41.790 --> 02:05:44.440
So I have I have mixed
feelings about that.

02:05:44.440 --> 02:05:48.690
And I would say
about that there are

02:05:48.690 --> 02:05:50.833
a number of intermediary
steps before you

02:05:50.833 --> 02:05:51.750
do your final project.

02:05:51.750 --> 02:05:54.160
There are a lot of small
things along the way.

02:05:54.160 --> 02:05:55.860
And you might
experiment with kind

02:05:55.860 --> 02:05:59.310
of both sides of that
coin, doing a topic

02:05:59.310 --> 02:06:01.840
and filming people that
you know very well.

02:06:01.840 --> 02:06:06.590
And then kind of taking yourself
out of that comfort zone.

